                new (
                    "00TQx000005cDhOMAU",
                    new UNOPSInteraction
                    {
                        Name = "Email: Notes from ED meeting with Sweden's Deputy Permanent Representative, 22. January 2023",
                        Type = InteractionType.Email,
                        Date = DateTime.Parse("2024-01-22").ToUniversalTime(),
                        Subject = "Email: Notes from ED meeting with Sweden's Deputy Permanent Representative, 22. January 2023",
                        Description = "To: plg.lo@unops.org; nyber@unops.org; jorge.moreiradasilva@unops.org; sonjalk@unops.org; hilaryb@unops.org; valeriek@unops.org; emiliep@unops.org; nicolej@unops.org; peterb@unops.org; victoriac@unops.org; hafidal@unops.org; tanvim@unops.org; draganmi@unops.org; cordulau@unops.org; annag@unops.org; oonaa@unops.org; sarahdg@unops.org; liliann@unops.org; olgad@unops.org; svene@unops.org; jawida@unops.org; thomasl@unops.org; vilhelmk@unops.org; naimoh@unops.org\nCC: \nBCC: \nAttachment: --none--\n\nSubject: Notes from ED meeting with Sweden's Deputy Permanent Representative, 22. January 2023\nBody:\nDear colleagues, \nEarlier today Jorge met with the Swedish Deputy Permanent Representative (Mr. Andreas von Uexküll) accompanied by ECOSOC ambassador (Ms.Charlotta Schlyter) and the caseworker (Ms. Anna Svensson). \nThe Swedish DPR pointed out that progress in recovering missing S3i funds and especially holding individuals responsible for the mismanagement were important points for Sweden. They therefore encouraged UNOPS to facilitate a confidential briefing done by OLA and UNOPS and to elaborate on how findings in the forensic audit would be handled. \nSweden appreciated Jorge's reassurance that UNOPS would stick to its original mandate as this had also been a grave concern for Stockholm.   \nThe Swedish DPR pointed out that the UNOPS management focus shifting the organisational culture and asked which concrete action points would be prioritized out of the many on the list presented. The staff surveys showed there was still a very long way to go to address the many personnel problems. Jorge reiterated the many cultural changes in the past 1.5 years and encouraged Member States to help shift the narrative about UNOPS away from crisis mode to focusing on the work being done on the ground. This would also help UNOPS colleagues who were just doing their jobs - and should not be blamed for past mistakes. \nFinally, upon a question from the Swedish DPR, Jorge explained the role of UNOPS in relation to Sigrid Kaag's new position in Gaza. \n***\nJorge initially complimented Sweden's active role in the Executive Board sessions which showed that they took the role very seriously. Sweden had been instrumental in getting UNOPS back in shape. Now only a few action points remained from the Comprehensive Response Plan which related to culture, risk and digitalisation. Jorge also underlined that UNOPS would continue reforming even beyond the CPR. Especially the culture shift would require special attention and it would take time. \nJorge also pointed out that UNOPS experienced an increasing demand from partners. For example UNOPS was hired for the Santiago network and to operationalise Sigrid Kaag's office in relation to the situation in Gaza. \nJorge mentioned that he would have liked to speak directly with the Secretary of State in Stockholm to clarify any questions or outstanding Swedish concerns.   \nThe DPR appreciated the direct outreach and presented 3 questions / comments to UNOPS:  From a Swedish perspective, accountability remained a key issue to be handled. It was important that the people involved in mismanagement in relation to S3i would be held accountable, and Sweden wanted to know when and how this would be handled - as the forensic audit would be taken forward.  Recovering the missing S3i funds was also a key issue and Sweden wanted to know how the work to recover the missing funds would be intensified.  Finally Sweden reiterated the request for more information on the investigation progress. They hoped that UNOPS could facilitate a confidential session together with OLA as a prolonged process would make matters even worse for all. Jorge agreed that it would be good to have a confidential briefing by OLA but could not promise such. UNOPS was in a difficult position where Jorge was getting all the questions while OLA was the unit in charge of the investigation. Jorge had spoken with OLA to try to facilitate a confidential briefing. \nWith regards to holding individual persons accountable, Jorge recalled that almost all senior managers had been replaced in the past 1.5 years. \nJorge underlined that everything UNOPS was currently doing was clearly within the original mandate - which was non-programmatic. This was appreciated by the DRP as this had also been a great concern for Sweden.  \nThe DPR then pointed out that the organisational culture shift was an important task for UNOPS and the KPMG recommendations should be taken seriously. The staff surveys were informative but the results showed that UNOPS still had a very long way to go. The responsibility for addressing the staff issues was clearly with the UNOPS management. Although Sweden appreciated the many changes in the past 1.5 years and the many initiatives presented, Sweden asked which key initiatives would be prioritised from the very long list.  \nJorge reiterated that although there were no silver bullet to fix the culture, many key issues had already been fixed. For example separation of powers between two DED, de-central decision makings and the involvement of regional directors in the management team. But other issues would go beyond that. \nTo sincerely change the organisational culture at UNOPS Jorge needed the help of Member States to change the narrative away from the crisis of the past. UNOPS staff was not to blame for a few individuals' misconduct so the narrative about UNOPS should focus on the work being done on the ground - which they should be proud of.  \nAsbjørn explained that HR colleagues had been analysing \"what\" issues had to be addressed and were now working hard on \"how\" to address the issues with specific actions which would be presented to Member States at a later stage. \nJorge pointed out that he did not find the continued critique of UNOPS to be fair. A lot had been done and a massive amount of reforms had been pushed through to address the issues. But still too much time was being spent on talking about the past which prevented UNOPS from moving on, and maintained the culture focus on past crisis. This was especially unfair to colleagues in the field who were just doing their job. Jorge hoped that Swedish colleagues in Stockholm would understand this and that he would be given an opportunity to speak with the State Secretary at some point.  \nThe DPR pointed to the Swedish government's zero tolerance for fraud and misconduct which was the reason behind the tough Swedish stance. The DPR assured Jorge about the Swedish recognition of the good work done by UNOPS colleagues on the ground.  \nThe DPR then asked for Jorge to elaborate about UNOPS' work with Sigrid Kaag in relation to Gaza - and what Member States could do to help. Jorge explained UNOPS' role and asked that Member States push for a ceasefire and independent verifications at the border - or at least a less restrictive approach by the Israelis as way too many items were rejected. \n\n/Asbjørn\n\nAsbjørn Brink | Head of Northern Europe Liaison Office | Partnerships and Liaison Group | UNOPS HQ | Copenhagen, Denmark | Mob: +45 40 80 36 54 | www.unops.org Keep up-to-date with UNOPS. Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube Subscribe to our external newsletter in English, French or Spanish.",
