                    "00TQx000005t5VnMAI",
                    new UNOPSInteraction
                    {
                        Name = "Email: Re: SDC and UNOPS",
                        Type = InteractionType.Email,
                        Date = DateTime.Parse("2024-01-29").ToUniversalTime(),
                        Subject = "Email: Re: SDC and UNOPS",
                        Description = "To: laraas@unops.org\nCC: rashak@unops.org; sophien@unops.org\nBCC: \nAttachment: --none--\n\nSubject: Re: SDC and UNOPS\nBody:\nDear LaraGreat, thanks for the update. Duly noted CHF 625 K is the amount being proposed. I hope SDC align with NOR regarding withdrawal of funds to UNWRAwill send email to Martin now and keep you in cc.Best wishes Louise\n\nOn Fri, 26 Jan 2024 at 16:10, Lara Abu Shilbayeh <laraas@unops.org> wrote:\nHi LouiseJust noting that the contribution amount in my email below is in dollarsThe CFH value is 625kThnx and bestLara\nOn Fri, 26 Jan 2024 at 15:44 Lara Abu Shilbayeh <laraas@unops.org> wrote:\nDear Louise,\nMany thanks for your support and taking the time for our call just now. Herewith the info we talked about, same as shared with you earlier, with some modifications, etc.Please feel free to use as you see fit in your communication with Bern.thanks again!Best,Lara\n\nSDC is interested in supporting the Access Support Unit (ASU) with a contribution of CHF 720K.\nUNOPS office is in discussion with the SDC office in Jerusalem, Ms Sabina Handschin (sabina.handschin@eda.admin.ch\nFirst Secretary, Development Cooperation & Humanitarian Aid in the Swiss Reps office about Potential funding)\nSDC have shared with UNOPS the global agreement between UNOPS and Switzerland and there are TWO main points that we would like to discuss further and see how they can be adapted in the agreement to fit the purpose/nature of the ASU being a highly political multi-donor project.\nThe two points are the following:\n1) The UNOPS indirect cost: The agreement stipulates an indirect fee of  3%. However, the ASU is a multi-donor project with the management fee fixed at 7%.Our understanding is that there is no interest in making the change. However, it is critical that we maintain a harmonized approach with all the donors, and have one budget for the project.To make the change, we would need to receive in writing from the SDC their approval to use 7% as mentioned in the related clause.\n“........the parties agree to apply a fixed indirect cost rate of 3% to new obligations under this Agreement unless and until a revised rate is approved in writing by the Funding Source.”\n2) The requirement for an evaluationConsidering the multi-donor nature of the support and the political context, any evaluation would require the buy-in of all donors under the leadership of UNSCO/UNOCHA, and the agreement on the intent/purpose of such an evaluation, which is also very likely to be a political discussion as well. This will require elaborate discussions with the relevant stakeholders, the timing for which might not be the best considering the ongoing conflict and access dynamics. All this would delay the conclusion of the funding agreement. We would suggest removing this evaluation from the agreement while relying on a continued flow of project monitoring data. Otherwise, while not ideal, we could agree with SDC on a 'lighter' mid-term review. My understanding is that our SDC focal point is with HQ on how to address the evaluation.\n\nOn Fri, Jan 26, 2024 at 1:52 PM Lara Abu Shilbayeh <laraas@unops.org> wrote:\nDear Louise,\nHappy Friday and hope all's well!\nFollowing on our earlier conversation re the Swiss contribution to the Access Support Unit, we have received the draft agreement from the SDC office here in Jerusalem. It does follow the globally agreed template - and so - there were two points that we have raised to the Swiss. I wanted to share with you a brief on the ongoing discussions.\n1) the management fee is at 3% as per the agreement. As the ASU is a multi-donor project, and the management fee has been fixed at 7%, we checked with the SDC about the possibility of receiving from HQ in writing that the project can maintain the 7%. However, SDC prefer to keep the 3%.So, we will be sharing with them an updated budget that reflects this fee.It seemed to be that this was the end of the conversation, but please let us know what you think about this.\n2) The other point is that the agreement stipulates in one of its clauses an \"evaluation\". Considering the multi-donor nature of the support, and the fact that any evaluation would require the buy-in of all donors under the leadership of UNSCO, also taking into account the current political situation, our discussion with SDC is to try to either remove this evaluation altogether (it seems this is not very much favored), or replace it with a 'lighter' mid-term review. I understand that the SDC local focal point is checking with HQ on that.What are your thoughts on that?\nI wanted to flag these two points to you and see what you think, what's your take on this, and if you have any suggestions or can have a nudge with the Swiss colleagues at HQ as well.\nThanks much in advance!Best regards,Lara\nOn Wed, Nov 8, 2023 at 11:57 AM Lara Abu Shilbayeh <laraas@unops.org> wrote:\nHi Louise,As this is a multi-donor project, the fees are set at 7%, and we follow a master budget that is approved internally (HQ) and accepted by the donors.\nBest,Lara\nOn Wed, Nov 8, 2023 at 11:56 AM Louise Jocelyn LEECH <louisel@unops.org> wrote:\nHi LaraGreat thanks for letting me know. Did you use the fee setting calculator - and if yes what was the fee we applied? This is a key conversation with Switzerland now. Thanks ! Best wishes , Louise \nOn Wed, 8 Nov 2023 at 10:49, Lara Abu Shilbayeh <laraas@unops.org> wrote:\nDear Louise,\nMany thanks for your support.The Swiss seem keen on providing support, they have come back with a lot of questions on the budget and we are providing responses as much as possible.I'll keep you updated and reach out in case we need your support again.\nKind regards,Lara\nOn Wed, Nov 8, 2023 at 10:34 AM Louise Jocelyn LEECH <louisel@unops.org> wrote:\nDear LaraJust a quick follow up to see what the latest situation is regarding the potential Swiss/SDC contribution?Thanks for letting me know and I do hope you are all doing OK despite the terrible ongoing situation in the region.All best wishes Louise \nLouise Leech, MPH  | Head of Geneva Liaison Office | Partnerships and Liaison Group | Mobile: +41 79 343 1614  UNOPS / Office des Nations Unies pour les Services Unité de Gestion du Projet\nGlobal project overview here  |  Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube \n\nOn Fri, Oct 20, 2023 at 6:05 PM Lara Abu Shilbayeh <laraas@unops.org> wrote:\nDear Louise,\nSuper thanks for making the time today for our quick call, and for sharing this information. It's very useful for us.\nHerewith the contact person at the SDC local office with whom the team has engaged on the ASU (access support unit):\nSabina Handschin\nsabina.handschin@eda.admin.chFirst Secretary, Development Cooperation & Humanitarian Aid in the Swiss Reps office about Potential funding. \n\nThe Access Support Unit (ASU) is operated by UNOPS on behalf of UNSCO and OCHA, and works towards advocacy and coordination of access of people and goods. Within the background of the ongoing escalation, the functions of the ASU are more critical. \nOur team is in touch with Sabina to seek humanitarian funding towards the ASU. Sophie and I hope to reach out to Sabina next week. We will consider the information you shared with us.\nThanks again,Best regards,Lara\nOn Fri, Oct 20, 2023 at 6:10 PM Louise Jocelyn LEECH <louisel@unops.org> wrote:\nDear LaraGreat to speak with you indeed.Please see below, we have signed a much smaller amount in 2023 than the two previous years, however, SDC are still engaging with us.My counterpart in Berne (SDC Headquarters) is Martin BORGEAUD, Senior Advisor, UN and MDB Section, FDFA, SDC.Do let me know if you wish me to join any conversations. Feel free to let me know Sabine's full details and I will write to Martin to see if we can encourage their engagement.Finally, my advice would be, don't be too pushy with them, but do reassure them that Martin (our Switzerland representative on UNOPS Executive Board) is following the implementation of the comprehensive response plan, and has full access to our Partners Platform to monitor Swiss contributions to UNOPS related projects.Reach out any time and please take care.Thoughts are with you allbest wishes Louise \n\nLouise Leech, MPH  | Head of Geneva Liaison Office | Partnerships and Liaison Group | Mobile: +41 79 343 1614  UNOPS / Office des Nations Unies pour les Services Unité de Gestion du Projet\nGlobal project overview here  |  Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube \n\n\n-- \n \n\nLara Abu Shilbayeh | Partnerships Advisor |\nJerusalem Operations Centre | Cell: +972 (0) 54-5892-505 |\n\n\nKeep\nup-to-date with UNOPS. Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube Subscribe to our external\nnewsletter in English, French or Spanish\n\n\n\n\n\n-- \n \n\nLara Abu Shilbayeh | Partnerships Advisor |\nJerusalem Operations Centre | Cell: +972 (0) 54-5892-505 |\n\n\nKeep\nup-to-date with UNOPS. Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube Subscribe to our external\nnewsletter in English, French or Spanish\n\n\n\n\n\n-- \n \n\nLara Abu Shilbayeh | Partnerships Advisor |\nJerusalem Operations Centre | Cell: +972 (0) 54-5892-505 |\n\n\nKeep\nup-to-date with UNOPS. Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube Subscribe to our external\nnewsletter in English, French or Spanish</sp",
                        Location = null,
