                ),
                new (
                    "00TQx000005tSx9MAE",
                    new UNOPSInteraction
                    {
                        Name = "Email: SDC funding for the Access Support Unit (Gaza)",
                        Type = InteractionType.Email,
                        Date = DateTime.Parse("2024-01-29").ToUniversalTime(),
                        Subject = "Email: SDC funding for the Access Support Unit (Gaza)",
                        Description = "To: martin.borgeaud@eda.admin.ch\nCC: laraas@unops.org; emiliep@unops.org\nBCC: \nAttachment: --none--\n\nSubject: SDC funding for the Access Support Unit (Gaza)\nBody:\nDear Martin\nGreetings from chilly Geneva - I hope this finds you well. Further to my previous email on this subject, I wanted to quickly follow up - and I would be happy to have a call this week if that's easier for you.\nAs you know SDC is interested in supporting the Access Support Unit (ASU) for Gaza with a contribution of CHF 625,000.Our local UNOPS office is in discussion with Ms Sabina Handschin (sabina.handschin@eda.admin.ch First Secretary, Development Cooperation & Humanitarian Aid in the Swiss Reps office in Jerusalem) in this regard. \nAs you know, we have our global agreement in place between UNOPS and Switzerland, however, there are two points that we would like to discuss further to see how they can be adapted to fit the purpose/nature of the ASU, being a highly political, multi-donor project.\n1) The UNOPS indirect cost: \nThe agreement stipulates an indirect fee of 3%. However, the ASU is a multi-donor project with a fixed management fee of 7% for all partners. As we have seen in other agreements it is critical that we maintain a harmonized approach with all the donors, and have one budget for the project to maximise efficiencies in both management and reporting.To make the change, we would need SDC approval in writing to apply the 7% as mentioned in the related clause in the agreement (I believe we did this in our Serbia agreement).\n2) The requirement for an evaluationConsidering the multi-donor nature of this support and the political context, any evaluation would require the buy-in of all donors under the leadership of UNSCO/UNOCHA, and the agreement on the intent/purpose of such an evaluation. This will require elaborate discussions with the relevant stakeholders, the timing for which might not be the best considering the ongoing conflict and access dynamics. All this would delay the conclusion of this very time sensitive funding agreement. We would suggest removing this evaluation from the agreement while relying on a continued flow of project monitoring data. Alternatively, while not ideal, we could agree with you on a 'lighter' mid-term review. \nLooking forward to hearing from you soon.All best wishesLouise\nLouise Leech, MPH  | Head of Geneva Liaison Office | Partnerships and Liaison Group | Mobile: +41 79 343 1614  UNOPS / Office des Nations Unies pour les Services Unité de Gestion du Projet\nGlobal project overview here  |  Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube",
                        Location = null,
                        GmailThreadId = null,
                        GmailMessageId = "00TQx000005tSx9MAE",
                        EmailAddresses = new List<string> { "sabina.handschin@eda.admin.ch", "martin.borgeaud@eda.admin.ch", "emiliep@unops.org", "laraas@unops.org" },
