                new (
                    "00TQx000006WJYvMAO",
                    new UNOPSInteraction
                    {
                        Name = "Email: Italy's Piano Mattei for Africa",
                        Type = InteractionType.Email,
                        Date = DateTime.Parse("2024-02-06").ToUniversalTime(),
                        Subject = "Email: Italy's Piano Mattei for Africa",
                        Description = "To: dalilag@unops.org; jakobt@unops.org; mariasalazar@unops.org; nathaliea@unops.org; rainerf@unops.org; tatianaw@unops.org; WorkneshG@unops.org\nCC: emiliep@unops.org; JeromeDT@unops.org; LaetitiaK@unops.org\nBCC: \nAttachment: --none--\n\nSubject: Italy's Piano Mattei for Africa\nBody:\nDear colleagues,As you may know, on 29 January Italy (current Presidency of the G7) held the Italy-Africa Summit in Rome, gathering 25 Leaders from Africa.The so-called Piano Mattei was launched.---Here is a summary:It includes 5.5 BIL EUR of which 3 BIL EUR from the Italian Climate fund and 2,5 BIL EUR from the Cooperation to Development fund. The idea is also to mobilize more funds through the Banks (Cassa Depositi e Prestiti, EIB etc.), the EU and other donors.PILOT PROJECTS IN 9 COUNTRIES - At least 9 African countries are involved in pilot projects: Morocco, Tunisia, Algeria, Egypt, Côte d’Ivoire, Ethiopia, Kenya, Democratic Republic of Congo and Mozambique.EDUCATION AND TRAINING - The Plan intends to promote the training of teachers, the launch of new professional courses in line with the needs of the labor market and collaboration with companies, involving in particular Italian operators and exploiting the Italian SMEs mode. For example, the Prime Minister announced the creation in Morocco of \"a large center of excellence for professional training on the topic of renewable energy\", while in Tunisia, a structural reform of schools and exchanges between students and teachers are planned as early as 2024.\nAGRICULTURE - The aim is to reduce malnutrition rates, encourage the development of agri-food chains, support the development of non-fossil bio-fuels. For Algeria \"a project of satellite monitoring on agriculture\" is planned; in Mozambique \"an agri-food center that enhances the excellence and export of local products\", while in Egypt the Plan intends to \"support in an area which is 200 kilometers far from Alessandria the production of wheat, soya, corn and sunflower, with investments in machinery, seeds, technologies and new cultivation methods\".HEALTH - The Plan aims at strengthening health systems, improving the accessibility and quality of primary maternal and child services (objective of a project in Côte d'Ivoire), to strengthen local capacities in terms of management of health personnel and research; develop strategies for preventing and containing health threats, in particular pandemics and natural disasters.ENERGY - It is one of the central sectors of the Plan with the aim of making Italy an energy hub, a bridge between Europe and Africa, as with \"the Elmed electrical interconnection between Italy and Tunisia and the new corridor for the transport of hydrogen from North Africa to Central Europe via Italy\". The climate-energy connection is central, with interventions to strengthen energy efficiency and the use of renewable energy. It is a commitment that will also include the development of technologies applied to energy and innovation centers, where Italian companies will be able to select local start-ups and thus support employment. This pillar includes \"an initiative in Kenya dedicated to the development of the biofuels supply chain, which aims to involve up to approximately 400 thousand farmers by 2027\".WATER - The interventions include the drilling of wells, powered by photovoltaic systems, and investments in distribution networks. For example, one of the projects for DRC involves the \"construction of wells and water distribution networks, especially for agricultural purposes, powered exclusively by renewable energy\", while in Ethiopia there will be \"water purification interventions\".———I suggest that you reach out to the Embassies/AICS offices in the listed countries and see how you could help.I know some of you are already well positioned but please let us know as we could support also through our current outreach to the Banks.Please keep us in the loop so that we can further support at our bestArrivederciCarmenMariacarmen Colitti | Head of Office and Representative | Partnerships and Liaison Group | Brussels Liaison Office | Brussels, Belgium | Tel: + 32 2 7881840| www.unops.org Keep up-to-date with UNOPS. Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube. \nSubscribe to our external newsletter in English, French or Spanish.",
                        Location = null,
                        GmailThreadId = null,
                        GmailMessageId = "00TQx000006WJYvMAO",
                        EmailAddresses = new List<string> { "laetitiak@unops.org", "tatianaw@unops.org", "dalilag@unops.org", "jakobt@unops.org", "emiliep@unops.org", "workneshg@unops.org", "mariasalazar@unops.org", "rainerf@unops.org", "jeromedt@unops.org", "nathaliea@unops.org" },
