                new (
                    "00TQx00000AfgyaMAB",
                    new UNOPSInteraction
                    {
                        Name = "Email: Re: Background information on Denmark and Norway.",
                        Type = InteractionType.Email,
                        Date = DateTime.Parse("2024-05-17").ToUniversalTime(),
                        Subject = "Email: Re: Background information on Denmark and Norway.",
                        Description = "To: victoriak@unops.org\nCC: \nBCC: \nAttachment: --none--\n\nSubject: Re: Background information on Denmark and Norway.\nBody:\nDear Victoria, \nYou are very welcome. Let us know when you have a slide deck or concept note that we can share with the migration ambassador. \nHave a lovely weekend. \nBest, Naimo\n\nOn Thu, May 16, 2024 at 6:09 PM Victoria Chendeka <victoriak@unops.org> wrote:\nDear Naimo,\nThis is great, thank you so much! We will follow up with our Accra colleagues for a meeting with the Embassies.Best regards,Victoria Chendeka | Communications Senior Analyst - Brand and Design| Africa Region| Ghana Multi-Country Office | Freetown, Sierra Leone |Tel: +232-30-882151/030307839 |  www.unops.org Keep up-to-date with UNOPS. Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube Subscribe to our external newsletter in English, French or Spanish. Check out our latest video: https://bit.ly/48d75EG\n\nOn Thu, May 16, 2024 at 1:52 PM Naimo HASSAN HIRSI <naimoh@unops.org> wrote:\nDear Victoria and Sofia, \nThank you for a very productive meeting. It was really nice meeting you. \nAs promised, I have prepared some background information about Norway and Denmark. As Asbjørn mentioned, we do see a great potential for increased engagement with both Denmark and Sweden. \nKey figures for Norway \nUSD 5.2 billion on ODA in 2022, 0,86 og the GNI having spent approximately 1% of GNI on ODA yearly since 2009, 3rd largest provider in terms of percentage of GNI. Norway's development policy is based on the Sustainable Development Goals adopted by all UN member states in 2015. The following areas are given priority; Education, Health, Climate change, the environment and the oceans, Private sector development, agriculture and renewable energy Humanitarian aid.\nSubstantial regional focus on Africa with USD 831.7 million in 2021. In 2021, the Norwegian MoFA launched the 2021-2025 strategies for Norway’s efforts in the Sahel region. The strategy's primary focus is on peace, stability and development in the Sahel region. Yesterday (May 15, 2024), Norway launched a five year strategy for their humanitarian assistance policy where the main themes are fighting poverty and climate action.\nMine action: Norway is increasing its contribution to Mine action with NOK 200 million. The Norwegian Government had already allocated NOK 164 million for mine clearance activities. The country is the 6th largest contributor to mine action. \nPartnership with Norway 2021 Procurement:  USD 59,31 million. 77th largest supplier in the UN system Delivery by UNOPS:  2024 (YTD): USD 1.6M 2023: USD 7.3 million, 2022: USD 2,6 million, 2021: USD 18,8 million, 2020: USD 5.3 million, 2019: USD 12.2 million  Engagement with UNOPS: 10 active engagements\n\nKey Figures for Denmark \nUSD 2,9 billion on ODA in 2022, 0.70% of GNI, 5th largest ODA provider in terms of percentage of GNI, having met the UN target of 0.70% of GNI since 1978. Denmark is committed to maintaining its ODA level at 0.70% of GNI.\nThe Danish government has strategically chosen to bring together the two areas; Development Cooperation and Global Climate Policy under one ministry. The dual ministerial role aligns with UNOPS’ mandate and efforts to promote sustainable development and address climate change.\nDenmark's development two main priorities as set out in the The World we Share’  areTo create hope and future opportunities, and provide better help to more people where it is most difficult – in regions neighboring humanitarian crisis and conflict zones, and in fragile states; To take the lead in the fight for climate, nature and environment, which means fulfilling its international climate commitments, including climate financing. \nSubstantial regional focus on Africa with USD 708,1 million in 2021. In August 2024, Denmark will launch a new partnership strategy with Africa. Denmark announced that DKK 400 million will be allocated to fight irregular migration in 2024 and has allocated a total budget of DKK 1.1 million between 2024-2029\n Partnership with Denmark:  Delivery: 2024 (YTD): 2,4K 2023: $16.2M; 2022: $829.3K; 2021: $2.1M; 2020: $2.3M.2022 UN Procurement: USD 1.30 billion.  Making Denmark the 4th largest supplier in the UN system. Recently, UNOPS has engaged with Denmark on rebuilding Ukraine. We also have engagements with Denmark in India on the Strategic Advisory and Technical Assistance for the Water and Sanitation project in India that also won UNOPS project of the year. Furthermore, UNOPS has built a migration center in Somalia and is currently providing technical assistance in passport production for Somalia on behalf of Denmark\nPlease let me know if you need further information on Denmark and Norway. You can also find more information on both countries and other Northern European partners on our Intranet page. \nBest regards, Naimo\n-- \n\nNaimo Hassan Hirsi | Partnerships Analyst | Partnerships and Liaison Group | UNOPS HQ | Copenhagen, Denmark | Mob: +45 26 83 48 78  | www.unops.org Keep up-to-date with UNOPS. Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube Subscribe to our external newsletter in English, French or Spanish.",
