                    "00TQx00000BLbBLMA1",
                    new UNOPSInteraction
                    {
                        Name = "Email: Re: UNOPS / Global Fund Climate Focus",
                        Type = InteractionType.Email,
                        Date = DateTime.Parse("2024-06-03").ToUniversalTime(),
                        Subject = "Email: Re: UNOPS / Global Fund Climate Focus",
                        Description = "To: Seonmi.Choi@theglobalfund.org\nCC: Philippa.DobreeCarey@theglobalfund.org; daniel.delatorre@theglobalfund.org\nBCC: \nAttachment: --none--\n\nSubject: Re: UNOPS / Global Fund Climate Focus\nBody:\nDear Seon MiIt was great to finally meet you at the WHA77 side event on Financing climate and health last week. Thanks for the great conversation and looking forward to discussing the further operationalizing of support to countries to improve sustainability / renewables in health infrastructure and procurement systems.I will be at GF on Wednesday afternoon if you are aroundBest wishes Louise \n\nOn Mon, 13 May 2024 at 17:34, Louise LEECH <louisel@unops.org> wrote:\nDear Seon Mi\n\nI hope this email finds you well - and I am sorry we didn't yet have an opportunity to meet in person - I hope we can remedy this soon!\nThis message is to provide some further clarification on what UNOPS is doing in the Climate and Environment space, for your information.\nWe have over 80 country offices that deliver USD 3.5 billion per year on behalf of our partners. This represents around 1,000 projects annually, that we deliver aligned with our mandate of Procurement, Infrastructure and Project Management. Over 30% of that delivery is now for Health (so add \"health\" to those 3 service lines and you get the idea!)  Over 50% of all projects are delivered in fragile settings, related to climate, conflict, security etc\nAll projects we deliver are needs driven - i.e. the Govt (typically but can be UN agency, international agency) and/or funding partner ask us for support to expand their own operational capacity.  Demand continues to increase and we are delivering more Climate-focused initiatives, such as climate-sensitive construction / rehabilitation works, more energy efficient procurement solutions (solar, mini grids, hybrid/lower energy generators) - all with a focus on sustainability and capacity building as key elements of the projects delivered. \nRegarding Myanmar, which I understand you wanted some more specific information on, we have been working there for decades and since 2020 with 23 partners, and have delivered a total of USD 1.2 billion in support, primarily in health, but also livelihoods and conflict resolution, and some small projects related to climate and environment.\nThe links here and below will give you some further insights which I am happy to discuss any time, including some of our colleagues who have been directly involved with relevant projects if this is useful. Best wishes Louise\nUNOPS climate projects in YemenInfrastructure for climate actionWater, Environment and Climate cluster\n\nOn Thu, 14 Mar 2024 at 20:47, Louise LEECH <louisel@unops.org> wrote:\nHi Daniel and Seonmi!\nHope all is well with you both. I was so sorry I wasn’t able to join the call you had with Erin. I was keen to follow up on Mark Edington’s suggestion of a short “info share/update” on ongoing Climate resilience initiatives both sides. I believe the wires may have been crossed regarding the purpose of the call!Can we please have a short call Friday 15th to clarify?!Many thanks and speak soonBest wishes Louise\nLouise Leech, MPH  | Head of Geneva Liaison Office | Partnerships and Liaison Group | Mobile: +41 79 343 1614  UNOPS / Office des Nations Unies pour les Services Unité de Gestion du Projet\nGlobal project overview here  |  Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube",
                        Location = null,
                        GmailThreadId = null,
