                new (
                    "00TQx00000BePZUMA3",
                    new UNOPSInteraction
                    {
                        Name = "Email: Notes from ED and DED meeting with Norway's Permanent Representative to the UN, Ms. Merete Fjeld Brattested, June 5",
                        Type = InteractionType.Email,
                        Date = DateTime.Parse("2024-06-10").ToUniversalTime(),
                        Subject = "Email: Notes from ED and DED meeting with Norway's Permanent Representative to the UN, Ms. Merete Fjeld Brattested, June 5",
                        Description = "To: nyber@unops.org; eo.cph@unops.org; plg.lo@unops.org; sonjalk@unops.org; emiliep@unops.org; hilaryb@unops.org; nicolej@unops.org; thomasl@unops.org; peterb@unops.org; annag@unops.org; oonaa@unops.org; sarahdg@unops.org; naimoh@unops.org; alexandram@unops.org\nCC: jorge.moreiradasilva@unops.org; kirstined@unops.org\nBCC: \nAttachment: --none--\n\nSubject: Notes from ED and DED meeting with Norway's Permanent Representative to the UN, Ms. Merete Fjeld Brattested, June 5\nBody:\nDear colleagues, \nOn June 5 the ED and DED met with the Permanent Representative of Norway to the UN (PR), Ms. Merete Fjeld Brattested. \nThe PR expressed admiration for the UNOPS reform process and assured the ED that Norway was working to get the progress reflected in a statement at the EB by a large group of cross-regional Member States.   \nNorway agreed on the need to direct requests for progress in accountability issues and retrieval of missing funds towards the correct UN entities which were the OLA and OIOS. \nThe ED briefed the PR about the work done by UNOPS in Afghanistan, Gaza, Myanmar and Ukraine which the PR found interesting and relevant to Norway's development aid priorities. \n\n***\nThe ED initially updated the PR about the status of UNOPS reforms, the culture roadmap, ongoing process of holding responsible individuals accountable for S3i and recovery of missing funds. The ED argued that UNOPS was stuck between a rock and a hard place as the ongoing proceedings for holding responsible individuals accountable and retrieving the missing funds were outside the control of UNOPS as OIOS and OLA were in charge of the processes. \nThe PR agreed with the ED's considerations and would touch base with those Member States that continued to push for UNOPS to provide updates and progress on accountability. However due to the Norwegian nationality of the former ED, this was a particularly delicate matter for Norway - as some Member States now also questioned investigations into the former ED. Norway had no interest in protecting the former ED even if she was a Norwegian national. They would therefore \"go with the flow\" of the EB in terms of the accountability issues at hand. \nThe PR stressed that Norway had great admiration for the very comprehensive reforms that had taken place in UNOPS over the past two years. A statement by a large group of Member States that crossed the usual regional groups was being prepared that clearly recognised the progress made. Norway was pushing internally among the Member States to ensure that this would be clearly recognised - against some, few Member States (Switzerland, The Netherlands and Canada) that still had a clear focus on accountability issues outside the control of UNOPS. Norway - together with Denmark and the UK - was actively trying to counter these in the WEOG internal discussions and tried to be very precise in the draft decision language on which UN entities accountability requests should be directed towards. \nThe ED then briefed the PR about the work done by UNOPS in Afghanistan, Gaza, Myanmar and Ukraine which the PR found interesting and relevant to Norway's development aid priorities. \nFinally the PR informed that Norway together with Portugal and other Member States were preparing next year's high level conference on financing for sustainable development in Spain - where Norway tried to push for recognition that this was about more than assembling more funds - but also instilling a greater focus on eg. implementation. The ED stressed the need to address the implementation gap which the PR agreed on. \n\nBest,Asbjørn\nAsbjørn Brink | Head of Northern Europe Liaison Office | Partnerships and Liaison Group | UNOPS HQ | Copenhagen, Denmark | Mob: +45 40 80 36 54 | www.unops.org Keep up-to-date with UNOPS. Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube Subscribe to our external newsletter in English, French or Spanish.",
