                ),
                new (
                    "00TQx00000CJ6kMMAT",
                    new UNOPSInteraction
                    {
                        Name = "Email: Re: An invitation to UNOPS chief Mr. Jorge Da Silva as a speaker at Narrating Global Conflict Conference",
                        Type = InteractionType.Email,
                        Date = DateTime.Parse("2024-06-25").ToUniversalTime(),
                        Subject = "Email: Re: An invitation to UNOPS chief Mr. Jorge Da Silva as a speaker at Narrating Global Conflict Conference",
                        Description = "To: nfs@hum.ku.dk\nCC: naimoh@unops.org\nBCC: \nAttachment: --none--\n\nSubject: Re: An invitation to UNOPS chief Mr. Jorge Da Silva as a speaker at Narrating Global Conflict Conference\nBody:\nDear Nicol\nApologies for my very late response! Your email simply drowned in my inbox. I am sorry for this. \nThank you very much for your kind invitation. Unfortunately Undersecretary General, Mr. Jorge Moreira Da Silva is unable to participate in the conference. I hope that we might be able to collaborate on another occasion. \nBest regards,Asbjørn\nOn Mon, 3 Jun 2024 at 11:39, Nicol Marie Foulkes Savinetti <nfs@hum.ku.dk> wrote:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nDear Asbjorn Brink\n \nI am writing in my capacity as the coordinator for the University of Copenhagen initiative,\nHUM:Global / Global Humanities and organizer of our forthcoming launch event.\nFor the launch entitled Narrating Global Conflict, we are planning a one-day conference with a slightly non-conventional program and would like to invite\nUNOPS chief Mr. Jorge Da Silva as a speaker. My contact at the UN,\nNajam UI Assar advised me to contact you in this regard.\n \nAccording to the (working) preliminary program, we would like to invite Mr Da Silva to be in conversation with one of the senior researchers at HUM:Global (see\nPeople – University of Copenhagen (ku.dk)) at 14:00 for 45-60 minutes. We are open to the suggestion of a different time in the bracket 12:00 – 16:00.\n \nPlease find a description of the theme below. We very much hope that Mr Da Silva will be enthusiastic about participating – the work of UNOPS is important to bring to this event, not least because of the global nature\n of the organization and the different perspectives it will bring to the discussion.\n \nNarrating Global Conflict\n \nAt a time when the world is ablaze with perilous conflagration, entrenched geopolitical struggles, and atrocious warfare – scholars, journalists, artists, activists, and civilians – whether up\n close or from afar – are trying to make sense of the senseless. \n \nScholars might try to uncover the roots or dynamics of a conflict, while journalists cover the events on the ground and the human experiences of the same. Artists articulate the conflicted emotions\n or utter hopelessness of warfare, while activists may mobilize to shine a light on the oppressed, the suffering or the persecuted, or to instill moral indignation and hope amidst carnage. And civilians, citizens, human beings – we absorb, we consume, we engage,\n we share, we debate, we reject, we ignore, we act. \nAll of us partake in globally interwoven narrations of conflict.\n \nThis public conference seeks to explore and connect how different professions narrate conflicts across time and space. It takes artistic expressions, personal accounts, and academic and professional\n analysis equally seriously. We do this both to unpack the innate qualities they possess as practices of sensemaking, and how they blend across a digitized, globalized interactive space to create sustained tropes of conflict and resolution in our time. Our\n aim, therefore, is sustain a dialogue between perspectives on global conflict at a historical moment when professional compartmentalization, ideological schisms and political naming and shaming risk tearing the critical, free, frank and interrogative exchange\n that universities are meant to lift up apart.\n \nNarrating global conflict is directed towards scholars and students at the University of Copenhagen and the wider interested audience. As such, all our activities are open to the public.\n \nI look forward to hearing from you at your earliest convenience.\n \nWarm regards\nNicol Marie Foulkes Savinetti\n \n \nNicol Savinetti, PhD\nHUM:Global // Global Humanities Coordinator\n \nUniversity of Copenhagen\nFaculty of Humanities\nForskning og Impact\nKaren Blixens Plads 8\nKøbenhavn S\n \nTEL +45 35 32 88 11\nDIR +45 40 90 12 59\nnfs@hum.ku.dk\n\nwww.globalhumanities.ku.dk\n\n\n\n\n \n \n\n \nHow\n we protect personal data\n \n \n \n \n\n\n\nAsbjørn Brink | Head of Northern Europe Liaison Office | Partnerships and Liaison Group | UNOPS HQ | Copenhagen, Denmark | Mob: +45 40 80 36 54 | www.unops.org Keep up-to-date with UNOPS. Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube Subscribe to our external newsletter in English, French or Spanish.",
