                ),
                new (
                    "00TQx00000DVljGMAT",
                    new UNOPSInteraction
                    {
                        Name = "Email: UN-SPBF & UNOPS collaboration - support scenarios and additional information",
                        Type = InteractionType.Email,
                        Date = DateTime.Parse("2024-07-19").ToUniversalTime(),
                        Subject = "Email: UN-SPBF & UNOPS collaboration - support scenarios and additional information",
                        Description = "To: shereen.zorba@un.org; rehana.velji@un.org; youssif.aboali@un.org\nCC: aureliear@unops.org; argitab@unops.org; jean-francoisl@unops.org; emiliep@unops.org\nBCC: \nAttachment: --none--\n\nSubject: UN-SPBF & UNOPS collaboration - support scenarios and additional information\nBody:\nDear Shereen, Rehana and Youssif,  \nMany thanks again for the discussion with our Geneva team. We are keen to find solutions to support you, so with thanks to the Geneva team, below are (1) some considerations for the potential scenarios, and (2) additional information related to hosting.\n\n1. Potential scenarios of support\n\nA. UNOPS provides regular support to UN SPBF: In this scenario, UNEP would keep the fiduciary role while UNOPS is in charge of procurement, HR, organising workshops etc. There is no guarantee that this would be cheaper than what UNEP is already charging you - managing multi-donor agreements is costly; we may even be more expensive. (UNOPS delivers services on a full cost recovery basis, and each engagement agreement between UNOPS and partner shall cover direct and indirect costs incurred by UNOPS in relation to the execution of the activities. Direct costs are determined using activity-based costing principles, while indirect costs are incurred by the management and administration of UNOPS activities and policies and charged as a fee percentage.)\n\nB. Joint hosting between UNOPS and UNEP: Given the size of the annual budget of Transformers, this solution is likely too costly, as UNEP and UNOPS would both charge you their costs.\n\nC. UNOPS hosting Transformers: In this scenario, your annual budget is not an issue, but UNOPS would need to obtain high level guarantees that there is full support for this solution on UNEP's side. Should this be identified as the suitable option, I stand ready to facilitate this, for example in the form of a high level exchange between our EDs on this matter. At this stage, without seeing your detailed budget, we cannot guarantee that we would be cheaper than UNEP - in fact, part of the process to assess the eligibility of a prospective hosted engagement is the review of the multi year work plan and budget. However, our management fees would be topped at 7%. In terms of connections with your broader work at UN-SPBF, we could explore options that would allow UNEP to maintain its normative function in Transformers' work, and where coordination between Transformers and UN-SPBF could continue.\n2. Additional information related to hosting\n\nA. Documentation: Please see attached a general overview of what being hosted by UNOPS means, which could be used for your board. In addition, our comprehensive guidelines on welcoming new hosted engagements are also attached. The key steps to join as a hosted entity are detailed on page 13. The Geneva team can walk you through the key steps during a call.\n\nB. Overview of the next steps:\n\n1. Supporting documents: To assess the eligibility and kick start the due diligence with our HQ, we would need the following from you: information related to any multi-year funding you have (donors, start and end date), your engagement workplan and budget, your board composition and the proposed way forward on the legal personality of Transformers (i.e. a paragraph describing your current legal status, how you are envisioning the process of your legal transformation and the role of the board). \n\n2. Board approval: We would need the official decision of your board to move towards UNOPS as a hosted entity to be able to start the proposal.\n\n3. Hosting Proposal and internal reviews: We need some more key documents such as your organigramme for the business analysis and the proposal. After the review of your key supporting documents (see a), we can start putting together the first version of the proposal. To give you an idea of what is required, please see here. We will need UN-SPBF and UNOPS to work on this jointly. In parallel, we will also work on internal reviews.\n4. Hosting agreement and other agreements: Development of Joint Hosting Agreement (JHA) and the Bilateral Legal Agreements.\n\nWe stand available should you have any questions, and are happy to organize a follow-up call at your convenience. Wishing you a lovely weekend in the meantime!\n\nAll the best, \nJean-François, Argita, Aurélie and Kajsa\n\n-- \nKajsa Hartman | Partnerships Specialist | Partnerships and Liaison Group | UNOPS Headquarters | Copenhagen, Denmark | www.unops.org Keep up-to-date with UNOPS. Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube. \nSubscribe to our external newsletter in English, French or Spanish.",
