                new (
                    "00TQx00000DoTFZMA3",
                    new UNOPSInteraction
                    {
                        Name = "Email: Questions regarding budget for Domanivka project",
                        Type = InteractionType.Email,
                        Date = DateTime.Parse("2024-07-29").ToUniversalTime(),
                        Subject = "Email: Questions regarding budget for Domanivka project",
                        Description = "To: vaszal@um.dk; annkah@um.dk\nCC: teresam@unops.org; mrasmu@um.dk; naimoh@unops.org; eleneag@unops.org; arunn@unops.org\nBCC: \nAttachment: --none--\n\nSubject: Questions regarding budget for Domanivka project\nBody:\nDear Anne and Vasyl, \nThank you for your email and inquiries regarding the budget for the Domanivka project. Apologies for my late response which is entirely my fault as I was away on holiday, \nI understand that your question related to the fact that the project itself costs about 950,000 USD but that the additional overhead costs amounted to a total of about 600,000 USD, which seemed excessive. While the estimated 14 month duration for the Domanivka project is in itself a rather complex project (see timeline here), the main reason for the costs is that most of it actually relates to the 3 months extension of the current infrastructure project. Thus, the lion's share of the 600K is attributable to the upcoming Arkas High School restoration project. Due to Domanivka's remoteness from Mykolaiv city, it necessitates addition of a driver for the duration of the project and an additional car, which upon completion will be handed over to the beneficiary, in agreement with you.The personnel structure includes addition of a dedicated WASH engineer for the duration of the project;Most importantly - as mentioned above, the duration of the project adds 3 extra months to the current duration of our common infrastructure project. This means that pending the extension of the Arkas project early next year, this amendment will already ensure the extension of the entire project team structure for extra 3 months. That project structure will of course be serving both Domanivka and Arkas functions, but we can not extend the project in only one part. Hence, this extension will also cover 3 extra months for the Arkas project.Finally, our Finance Team by mistake incurred an increased cost for the use of local and shared UNOPS services (from USD 6,500 to USD 6,681 per month) on the entire duration of the infrastructure project, while this should of course only be on the extended months. This reduces the overall costs with about 30K. To give an indication of the distribution of costs between the Arkas and Domanivka project, please find here a spreadsheet where Elene has kindly provided a detailed breakdown - and tried to make a split between the two projects. In the last column we showed the distribution of personnel costs 50%-50% between Domanivka and Arkas, which was the easiest way to make a split. But in fact, it could be assume that the real costs attributed to Arkas and Domanivka will be 60%-40% in favour of Arkas.\nWith a 50-50 split it means the the costs for the work attributable to Domanivka will be closer to 341,719 USD - rather than 600K. \nI hope this clarifies and we remain as always available for a meeting to discuss upon your convenience. \nBest,Asbjørn Asbjørn Brink | Head of Northern Europe Liaison Office | Partnerships and Liaison Group | UNOPS HQ | Copenhagen, Denmark | Mob: +45 40 80 36 54 | www.unops.org Keep up-to-date with UNOPS. Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube Subscribe to our external newsletter in English, French or Spanish.",
