>;\n Yacoub Ali El-Hillo <elhillo@un.org>; Dyfan Rhys Jones <dyfan.jones@un.org>; Eskedar Nega Woldekidan <eskedar.nega@un.org>; Sarwat Adnan <sarwat.adnan@un.org>; Simon John Hacker <hackers@un.org>; Yolanda Durant Mc Klmon <yolanda.durant-mcklmon@un.org>; Ana-Maria\n Lebada <ana-maria.lebada@un.org>; Anima Dua Agyeman <dua-agyeman@un.org>; Christopher Stokes <cstokes@un.org>; Daniel Safran-Hon <safran-hon@un.org>; Elisabeth Diaz Hochmann <diaz2@un.org>; Hyung Hak Nam <nam@un.org>; Jay Bahadur Shah <jay.shah@un.org>; Julie\n Morizet <julie.morizet@un.org>; Marie-Sophie Reck <reck@un.org>; Mark Schulman <schulman@un.org>; Nadia Hadi <hadin@un.org>; Sehen Hirpo <hirpo@un.org>; Svenja Angela Rauch <rauchs@un.org>; Zoubir Benarbia <zoubir.benarbia@un.org>; Ao Kong <konga@un.org>;\n Bianca Jinete <bianca.jinetemejia@un.org>; Brianna Harrison <brianna.harrison@un.org>; Carmen Arguello <carmen.arguello@un.org>; Chikezie Godwin Anyanwu <chikezie.anyanwu@un.org>; Chloe Ludden <chloe.ludden@un.org>; Christina Human <humanc@un.org>; Diloro\n Kadirova <kadirova@un.org>; George Abualzulof <george.abualzulof@un.org>; Gonzalo Pizarro <gonzalo.pizarro@un.org>; Hanna Stenbacka Koehler <hanna.stenbackakoehler@un.org>; Helena Fraser <fraser@un.org>; Janine Theresa Chase <chase@un.org>; Jasmina Belchovska-Tasevska\n <jasmina.belcovska@un.org>; Joerg Schimmel <jorg.schimmel@un.org>; Katarina L Kuai <katarina.kuai@un.org>; Kirit Patel <patelk@un.org>; Mao Kawada <mao.kawada@un.org>; Natia Cherkezishvili <natia.cherkezishvili@un.org>; Olga Yatskevich <yatskevich@un.org>;\n Poorti Sapatnekar <sapatnekar@un.org>; Rani Kantari <rani.elkantari@un.org>; Rodolpho Valente <valenter@un.org>; Romina Sta. Clara <romina.sta.clara@un.org>; Signe Jepsen <jepsen@un.org>; Simon Pierre Tegang <simonpierre.tegang@un.org>; Tala Chammas <tala.chammas@un.org>;\n Zhuoya Liu <zhuoya.liu@un.org>; Alix Nijimbere <nijimbere@un.org>; Ayano Tanaka <ayano.tanaka@un.org>; Michael Schaadt <michael.schaadt@un.org>; Narmina Guliyeva <narmina.guliyeva@un.org>; Rafael Beleboni <rafael.beleboni@un.org>; Valentine Hoschet-Verdier\n <hoschet-verdier@un.org>; Efren Osorio <efren.osorio@un.org>; Narissa Seegulam <narissa.seegulam@un.org>; Asel Abdurakhmanova <asel.abdurakhmanova@un.org>; Pauline Atieno Owuor Magawi <pauline.magawi@un.org>; Chakib Zouaghi <chakib.zouaghi@un.org>; Alper Almaz\n <hasan.almaz@un.org>; DCO-JSDGF Team <DCO-JSDGF-Team@un.org>\n\nSubject: Joint SDG Fund – Update on Funding Rounds with Guidance on the Integrated Policy and Capacity Building seed funding track\n\n\n \nDear Resident Coordinators, dear colleagues,\n \nI am pleased to share this update of Joint SDG Fund developments since we met on 5 March at the Global RC/UNCT clinic on the Fund 2.0.  Attached\n please find:  \n \n\n\nThe current list of potential fund allocations by pathfinder country and the criteria established by inter-agency processes for country selection of the four funding rounds launched to date, namely\n the Global Accelerator on Jobs and Social Protection, Digital Transformation, Food Systems Transformation, and SDG Localization. These processes reflect both the political momentum surrounding many of these priority areas as well as an enormous effort\n by the wider UN development system, including RCs, your UNCTs and colleagues across the agencies, funds and programs at HQ and regional levels, who have not only developed the criteria but undertaken the painstaking process of country selection. \n\n \n\nAs you recall, this list is preliminary and, in many ways, meant to foster pipeline development for the expansion of each of the Fund’s thematic tracks as the Fund is replenished. \n\n \n\nGuidance for the seed funding track on Integrated Policy and Capacity Building. This track seeks to provide seed\n funding of up to $250,000 to UN country teams, aimed at facilitating integrated policy services and scaled capacity building to incubate transformative solutions for key SDG transitions.\nIn alignment with the\nJoint SDG Fund’s 2023-2026 transitional strategy,\n joint programmes are intended to leverage the comparative advantages and capacities of the UN development system to propel key SDG transitions. Seed funding via the UNCT should support the sourcing, identifying and incubating of solutions and pulling together\n of expertise required to deliver them, be it from the government, private sector or civil society – or from a partner country that has undertaken a similar transition.\n \n\nThese seed funding proposals will have a maximum implementation duration of 12 months with the participation of two UN Organisations recommended. The\n joint programmes will be considered for review and approval on a rolling basis. While all UNCTs are invited to submit proposals for the Fund’s pipeline, countries ineligible in current funding tracks/rounds will be prioritised.\n\n \n\n\nSeed funding track ProDoc template and annexes to be submitted through the online form:\nhttps://forms.office.com/e/VVEY0FTZpz.\n The original webinar\n recording\n and  presentation\n are also here, for your reference.\n \nYour humble Joint SDG Fund Secretariat is standing by to support, whether a brainstorm call or to add further clarity on this or other funding tracks.\n Do reach out to me or via email to the Dream team at \njointsdgfund@un.org, sebastien.fahrni@un.org\nand\nheewoong.kim@un.org. \n \nWith best regards, \nLisa",
                        Location = null,
