                new (
                    "00TQx00000FvMuMMAV",
                    new UNOPSInteraction
                    {
                        Name = "Email: Re: USAID suggested, updated SP language for UNOPS in ADS 308mab",
                        Type = InteractionType.Email,
                        Date = DateTime.Parse("2024-09-18").ToUniversalTime(),
                        Subject = "Email: Re: USAID suggested, updated SP language for UNOPS in ADS 308mab",
                        Description = "To: svene@unops.org; asbjornb@unops.org\nCC: asbjornb@unops.org; emiliep@unops.org; vineshw@unops.org; elizabethdu@unops.org; iraklij@unops.org; alistairs@unops.org\nBCC: \nAttachment: --none--\n\nSubject: Re: USAID suggested, updated SP language for UNOPS in ADS 308mab\nBody:\nDear Colleagues:\nGood morning from Washington. I wanted to update you all of my meeting yesterday (Sept 17) with USAID regarding the sunsetting of the special clauses. While making it clear that this was unofficial and very preliminary, I shared with them the feedback from the meeting I had with FG on Monday (Sept 16). USAID said they would like to wrap this up quickly and sought to replicate the August 2024 INL language in their proposal. \nBelow are USAID's responses to the points discussed in the Sept 16  meeting. \n1) Do we need this under the special provisions or can this be in any other form, e.g. agreed templates or MoU?\n\nUSAID said that these clauses would be part of their ADS directives website and that they would be public.  USAID noted that they \"have to do this\" and that the agency does not rely on MOUs for projects.  I mentioned that UNOPS wanted to ensure that these provisions would not be presented in a way that makes it seem like the S3i crisis is recent or ongoing. USAID said they understood this. As such, they said they would recommend the agency's General Council put in a provision that requires a review after two years. \n2) Suggest to find one focal point for the excess reserve communication rather than every agreement officer.? \nUSAID said this was intended to be a focal point and not require a separate notification for each project or agreement.  My counterpart said he would talk to their lawyers and clarify this so that it was clear on where to send the required information. \n3) UNOPS will further provide a detailed explanation of the basis for the management fee rate[s] as per the approval of its Executive Board on a biennial basis.  \nUSAID seemed amenable to this and said they would consult with their finance team and lawyers. \nAs a path forward, USAID suggested I send them an official response with proposed revisions to the text. Once done, my main counterpart believed we could easily conclude this via email exchanges as he believed USAID would concur with our suggestions. I am losing my amazing counterpart on October 15 so I'd like to push this as fast as possible to see if we can leverage him to get this over the finish line. \n@Eli and Vinesh, I will wait to respond until LG has had a chance to review the proposed changes we discussed with FG. I have opened a ticket and will add this email to it. \nThanks again for all of your help and support in restoring a normal business relationship with the US. I can't wait to finish the final piece of the puzzle. In the meantime, please let me know if you have any questions or need additional information. \nBest regards, Patrick \nOn Mon, Sep 16, 2024 at 11:05 AM Michael Patrick ELLSWORTH <patrickel@unops.org> wrote:\n+Asbjorn BRINK \nOn Mon, Sep 16, 2024 at 11:05 AM Michael Patrick ELLSWORTH <patrickel@unops.org> wrote:\nSven:\nThanks so much! I will see USAID tomorrow and share our feedback informally. I will propose we meet with LG and their counterparts. UNGA HL week will likely delay this a bit on the USAID side. \nI will send everyone an update after my meeting with USAID.\nBest regards, Patrick \nOn Mon, Sep 16, 2024 at 10:19 AM Sven ECKERT <svene@unops.org> wrote:\nHi Patrick,thanks for the good meeting. Please find the suggested changes as per our discussion below in blue. Open for more input from LG if needed.BestSvenSven Eckert (he/his) | Head of Project Finance | Finance Group | UNOPS HQ | Copenhagen, Denmark | Tel: +45 4533 7559 | www.unops.org \nKeep up-to-date with UNOPS. Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube. \nSubscribe to our external newsletter in English, French or Spanish. \n\nOn Fri, Sep 13, 2024 at 11:45 PM Michael Patrick ELLSWORTH <patrickel@unops.org> wrote:\nDear Colleagues:\nHappy Friday! I wanted to send you the proposed language from USAID to sunset the temporary clauses and the three percent. I just looked at it, and it looks like the INL text. I want to give USAID some initial thoughts on this approach at my meeting on Tuesday. \nVinesh, Eli, Sven, and Irakli: Would it be possible to do a quick call on Monday? (I will look for a slot on the calendar.) If not, could you share some initial and informal thoughts or feedback for me to share with USAID? \nI want to capitalize on this as soon as possible as I am losing my great counterpart in mid-October. \nThanks for all of your work and support on this effort!\nHave a great weekend! \nPatrick \n---------- Forwarded message ---------\nFrom: Andrew Apostolopoulos <aapostolopoulos@usaid.gov>\nDate: Fri, Sep 13, 2024 at 5:19 PM\nSubject: USAID suggested, updated SP language for UNOPS in ADS 308mab\nTo: Michael Patrick ELLSWORTH <patrickel@unops.org>\nCc: Elizabeth Buckingham <ebuckingham@usaid.gov>, Alana Marsili <amarsili@usaid.gov>, Charles Kiamie III <ckiamie@usaid.gov>, Kaarli Sundsmo <ksundsmo@usaid.gov>\n\nHi Patrick:\nI hope that this message finds you well.  I wanted to get this out to you before the weekend, as I am sure you will need to pass this on to Copenhagen, and it would be good to have that feedback for our discussions on Tuesday.\nBelow, you will see USAID's DRAFT suggested language to replace the current UNOPS special provision language in ADS 308mab.  Please note, that this language is not definitive and is subject to change and that this email does not constitute approval of this draft language. \nRather, this language is being shared with your team to ensure that both USAID and UNOPS are on the same page before USAID moves forward with any formal action to alter the special provision language as it pertains to UNOPS in ADS 308mab.\nOur team looks forward to hearing back from UNOPS regarding this suggested way forward.  On a final note, I would greatly appreciate confirmation of receipt of this message. Have a great weekend!\nBest Regards,\nAndrew\nProposed Revised UNOPS Provision Language:(This provision is required for all new agreements with the United Nations Office for Project Services. This provision replaces the prior UNOPS special provision)RAA.15 Special Provision for the United Nations Office of Project Services (XX 2024)\nDo we need this under the special provisions or can this be in any other form, e.g. agreed templates or MoU? a. The parties agree that any funds provided under this Agreement for fees or costs categorized as “indirect costs,” “programme support costs,” or “management fees” which support the Recipient's indirect operational costs (hereinafter referred to as “management fees” in this provision) shall be used to support the Recipient's indirect operational costs in carrying out this Agreement. These management fees are established in accordance with the Biennial Budget Estimates approved by the UNOPS Executive Board, aiming for a net-zero revenue. If the net-zero revenue target is surpassed and UNOPS creates any excess reserves derived from the funds provided by USAID under this Agreement, UNOPS will promptly communicate Executive Board decisions regarding the excess reserves to the Agreement Officer. \nSuggest to find one focal point for the excess reserve communication rather than every agreement officer.  \nb. UNOPS shall provide USAID with a yearly financial statement, audited in accordance with the applicable financial regulations, rules, policies, and procedures of UNOPS, showing how management fees received by UNOPS  from USAID and its other funding sources have been applied to cover its indirect costs in the applicable year. UNOPS will further provide a detailed explanation of the basis for the management fee rate[s] as per the approval of its Executive Board on a biennial basis. applicable to USAID under this Agreement.\nc. Any proposed management fees higher than eight percent must be justified through a separate note provided by UNOPS headquarters confirming that the proposed management fee rate is derived from the approved cost recovery model approved by the UNOPS Executive Board. The cognizant Agreement Officer has the discretion to approve a proposed management fee over eight percent. If the Agreement Officer agrees to a management fee over eight percent, UNOPS must provide a copy of the justification along with the Agreement Officer’s approval  to the Bureau for Planning, Learning and Resource Management at pioperformance@usaid.gov.\n\n-- \nAndrew ApostolopoulosProgram OfficerOffice of Development CooperationUSAID | Bureau for Planning, Learning, and Resource Management+1 202 258 2969Pronouns: he, his, him\n \n\n-- \nPatrick Ellsworth | Senior Partnerships Advisor | Address: 1775 K Street NW, Washington, DC 20006 |Tel: +1 703 350 2932 | www.unops.org Keep up-to-date with UNOPS. Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube\n\n\n-- \nPatrick Ellsworth | Senior Partnerships Advisor | Address: 1775 K Street NW, Washington, DC 20006 |Tel: +1 703 350 2932 | www.unops.org Keep up-to-date with UNOPS. Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube\n\n-- \nPatrick Ellsworth | Senior Partnerships Advisor | Address: 1775 K Street NW, Washington, DC 20006 |Tel: +1 703 350 2932 | www.unops.org Keep up-to-date with UNOPS. Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube\n\n-- \nPatrick Ellsworth | Senior Partnerships Advisor | Address: 1775 K Street NW, Washington, DC 20006 |Tel: +1 703 350 2932 | www.unops.org Keep up-to-date with UNOPS. Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube",
                        Location = null,
                        GmailThreadId = null,
                        GmailMessageId = "00TQx00000FvMuMMAV",
                        EmailAddresses = new List<string> { "patrickel@unops.org", "aapostolopoulos@usaid.gov", "elizabethdu@unops.org", "amarsili@usaid.gov", "emiliep@unops.org", "pioperformance@usaid.gov", "ckiamie@usaid.gov", "ksundsmo@usaid.gov", "asbjornb@unops.org", "ebuckingham@usaid.gov", "svene@unops.org", "alistairs@unops.org", "iraklij@unops.org", "vineshw@unops.org" },
