                new (
                    "00TQx00000Fvae4MAB",
                    new UNOPSInteraction
                    {
                        Name = "Email: New U.S. Global Development Strategy",
                        Type = InteractionType.Email,
                        Date = DateTime.Parse("2024-09-19").ToUniversalTime(),
                        Subject = "Email: New U.S. Global Development Strategy",
                        Description = "To: PLG.Personnel@unops.org\nCC: \nBCC: \nAttachment: --none--\n\nSubject: New U.S. Global Development Strategy\nBody:\nDear Colleagues: I am writing to share some key insights from my attendance at the U.S. government’s new Global Development Strategy launch at the White House on September 18. President Biden’s National Security Advisor (NSA) Jake Sullivan and USAID Administrator Samantha Power introduced this update, the first since the Obama-Biden Administration's strategy in 2010. They both outlined the strategy's main priorities and how it addresses the ever-increasing global challenges we face today. (The White House Press Release on Global Development Strategy provides an excellent strategy summary. The complete strategy can be found here.)NSA Sullivan opened the event by highlighting the pressing global challenges, including wars, conflicts, pandemics, and climate issues. He emphasized that the new strategy demonstrates the vital role of development, alongside defense and diplomacy, in securing U.S. national interests and addressing global crises.He emphasized the importance of partnering with like-minded nations and international organizations to address global challenges. Although the U.S. is the largest bilateral donor, Sullivan noted that the required resources far exceed current funding levels, and there is a need to \"shift from billions to trillions.\" To achieve this, he highlighted the necessity of reforming international financial institutions (IFIs) to streamline their operations, leverage their balance sheets to expand funding, and seek capital increases from Congress when needed. He stressed the importance of mobilizing private sector capital to meet global development needs.Sullivan expressed pride in the G-7’s Partnership for Global Infrastructure and Investment, highlighting its transformative potential for countries and regions. He used the Lobito rail corridor in Africa as an example, a $1 billion U.S.-backed project poised to boost the economies of Angola, the Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC), and neighboring nations. Sullivan emphasized the importance of continuing such large-scale investments to drive sustainable development and regional growth through \"transparent initiatives,\" contrasting them with China’s Belt and Road Initiative (BRI).Administrator Power echoed many of National Security Advisor Sullivan's points, highlighting USAID's efforts to enhance development diplomacy by strengthening partnerships with donors, the UN, international financial institutions, and recipient countries. She noted that USAID has created an Office of the Chief Economist to evaluate programs, measure outcomes, and prioritize investments. Like Sullivan, Power emphasized the critical role of diplomacy, defense, and development in the U.S. national security strategy. She added that the administration must demonstrate how development spending delivers tangible value to U.S. taxpayers and companies.In addition, she emphasized USAID's commitment to engaging the private sector. She highlighted USAID’s $50 million EDGE Fund, which aims to incentivize private sector initiatives contributing to development outcomes.  (Edge Fund Press Release). Power noted that USAID is preparing another round of EDGE funding. She also emphasized the USAID’s need to streamline its bureaucratic policies and encourage innovation. She also highlighted the importance of increasing USAID's spending with local suppliers, aiming to improve localization efforts beyond the current \"very low level\" of 10-11%.Following the remarks, a roundtable discussion took place featuring MCC Chair Alice Albright, DFC Deputy Director Nisha Biswal, Assistant Secretary of the Treasury for International Affairs Alexia Latortue, and Deputy Secretary of State for Management Richard Verma. The panelists shared insights on how their respective agencies are advancing key aspects of the Global Development Strategy. They expressed confidence in bipartisan support for development as a crucial national security tool for the U.S. In closing, they emphasized the importance of creativity and innovation among development professionals in tackling the world's most pressing challenges.The release of the new U.S. Global Development Strategy is an important resource for UNOPS as it consolidates the current administration's global development approach and priorities. It also demonstrates a strong White House commitment to elevating development alongside diplomacy and defense as a critical component of U.S. national security. The strategy’s relevance for UNOPS could be short-lived depending on the outcome of November’s election. If Vice President Harris wins, many current priorities and initiatives will likely continue. However, if former President Trump is elected, his administration would likely discard much of the plan, shift resources toward domestic priorities, and redirect remaining assistance away from efforts like combating climate change. I will continue monitoring the potential priorities of both Democratic and Republican candidates as we approach November’s election and will provide periodic updates this fall. In the meantime, please don’t hesitate to reach out if you have any questions, need further information, or if there’s anything I can do to support your engagement with U.S. partners.Have a great weekend! Best regards, Patrick --\nPatrick Ellsworth | Senior Partnerships Advisor | Address: 1775 K Street NW, Washington, DC 20006 |Tel: +1 703 350 2932 | www.unops.org Keep up-to-date with UNOPS. Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube",
                        Location = null,
                        GmailThreadId = null,
                        GmailMessageId = "00TQx00000Fvae4MAB",
                        EmailAddresses = new List<string> { "plg.personnel@unops.org" },
