                ),
                new (
                    "00TQx00000IvxsLMAR",
                    new UNOPSInteraction
                    {
                        Name = "Email: Re: SFA incident notification - urgent call",
                        Type = InteractionType.Email,
                        Date = DateTime.Parse("2024-12-04").ToUniversalTime(),
                        Subject = "Email: Re: SFA incident notification - urgent call",
                        Description = "To: gkassem1@worldbank.org\nCC: drubinos@worldbank.org; alistairs@unops.org; christinebo@unops.org; vmosoti@worldbank.org; bkuba@worldbank.org; akircher1@worldbank.org\nBCC: \nAttachment: --none--\n\nSubject: Re: SFA incident notification - urgent call\nBody:\nDear Gamila, \nI hope this message finds you both well.  As you know, UNOPS cannot comply with the World Bank's ESS reporting requirements for SEA incidents due to our adherence to a different UN reporting mechanism and our inability to commit to the timelines outlined in the Annex 1 of the new SFA template. Our process keeps SEA incident reports strictly confidential until they are disclosed to UN country leadership and the Secretary General's Office, after which basic information becomes publicly available to donors. This discrepancy has become a sticking point in our discussions. \nWe have invested some effort internally, working closely with our Internal Audit and Investigation Group, to develop a proposal we hope will address your concerns while taking into account our limitations. This proposal, which consists of the following steps, is inspired a solution considered by other UN agencies: \n1) SEA cases are reported by the Country Office (CO) to IAIG.2) IAIG will report all cases (received through the CO and directly) to the SG through the iTracker.3) IAIG will complete the early incident reporting form (as required by OSCEA). This form contains highly confidential information (allegations, name and DOB of victim).4) The CO will share the allegation number from the iTracker with GRS to inform them of the allegations and allow them to track the case's progress5) The CO will respond to GRS on any questions related to mitigating measures and operational matters6) IAIG will respond to GRS on follow-up high-level investigative questions. Regarding the timelines specified in Annex 1 for SEA notification and reporting, we would like to propose using the term \"promptly\" or \"as soon as practicable\" instead of fixed timelines. This adjustment would provide the necessary flexibility while assuring you of our commitment to timely reporting.  Alternatively, we propose the following language: \"the UN Partner will notify the Bank as soon as possible from the moment the credibility of the complaint has been ascertained, after any required preliminary measures have been put in place, and before the formal investigation has started\".  This language ensures that victims are prioritized, that preliminary measures are effectively implemented to protect, and that accurate information is provided to you in good time. \nUNOPS prioritizes a victim-centered approach in handling SEA incidents.  Our primary focus is on the safety, well-being and confidentiality of the victims. Rigid reporting deadlines can sometimes conflict with this approach.  One can think of a complaint received on a Friday afternoon that may not be reported within a 48-hours window due to limited weekend capacity.  Initial reports often contain minimal information, sometimes only a case number without details about the alleged perpetrator or victim.  Gathering accurate preliminary information can be time-consuming, especially in remote contexts where our projects are implemented.  These challenges are not due to internal policy issues, but stem from capacity constraints in our operating environment. We hope that this solution respects both our operational realities and your need for timely information. \nI look forward to your review on this proposal. If we are aligned on the process, we can look at the language that could be added to Annex 1 to reflect this approach. \nWarm regards, \nElizabeth \nOn Tue, Nov 5, 2024 at 3:00 PM Gamila Kassem <gkassem1@worldbank.org> wrote:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nDear Vinesh,\n \nThank you for taking the time to meet with us yesterday. Following our conversation with you and other UN agencies, we reverted to the previously agreed language on notification\n of incidents and accidents in the SFA template. This language will be reflected in the retrofitted SFAs. We have corporate endorsement to go ahead with the signature now.\n \nAll the best,\n \nGamila & Birgit\n \n \n\n\nFrom: Gamila Kassem\n\n\nSent: Monday, November 4, 2024 4:02 PM\n\nTo: Vinesh WINODAN <vineshw@unops.org>; Elizabeth DUBY <elizabethdu@unops.org>; christinebo@unops.org\n\nCc: Victor Bundi Mosoti <vmosoti@worldbank.org>; Birgit Kuba <bkuba@worldbank.org>; Dae Rubinos <drubinos@worldbank.org>\n\nSubject: SFA incident notification - urgent call\n\nImportance: High\n\n\n \nDear Vinesh, Elizabeth, and Christine,\n \nAfter further feedback received, we further tweaked the language on notification on incidents and accidents. See below suggested edits. Could we have a quick call on this ASAP?\n \nAll the best,\n \nGamila & Birgit\n \n\n\n\n\nINCIDENTS AND ACCIDENTS\n\n \na) Promptly notify\nthe Government and the Bank of any incident or accident related to the Delivery of Outputs which has, or is likely to have, a significant adverse effect on the environment, the affected communities,\n the public or workers, including, inter alia, cases of sexual exploitation and abuse (SEA), sexual harassment (SH), and accidents that result in death or serious or multiple injury.\n\n \nFor SEA/SH incidents, the notification and any follow up reporting to the Bank shall be shared with\n the Bank’s corporate Grievance Redress Service (GRS).  For any other incidents or accidents, the notification and any follow up reporting to the Bank shall be shared with the Bank task team.\n\n \nUN Partner will report alleged incidents of SH involving UN Partner Staff and Non-Staff Personnel\n through its Office of Internal Audit and Investigations’ annual reports to the UN Partner Executive Board, which are made publicly available.\n \nb)\nSubsequently, provide\na report to the Government and the Bank with sufficient detail regarding the scope, severity, and possible causes of the incident or accident, indicating immediate measures taken or that are planned\n to be taken to address it, and any information provided by any Consultant or Contractor, as appropriate.\n\n \nc)\nIn cases where protection concerns or applicable UN Partner rules may limit such notification or information that can be reported to the Government, the UN Partner shall promptly only notify the Bank. After\n consultations with the Bank on the modalities, extent of information to be shared and timeline of the notification to the Government of the incident or accident, notify the Government accordingly. In exceptional cases, where protection concerns outweigh the\n benefit of such notification, or applicable UN Partner rules prohibit any sharing of information, the Bank and the UN Partner may agree to not notify the Government of that incident or accident.\n \nd) At the Bank’s request, share any Consultant or Contractor notification and report, redacted to\n remove Personal Data. \n\n\n \na) Notify\nthe Government and the Bank no later than 48 hours after learning of the incident or accident.\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nb)         Provide the report on SEA/SH incidents within 10 days of the notification, and the report on any other incident or accident\n within 30 days of the notification. Depending on the circumstances, this timeframe may be extended in writing.\n\n \nc)        \nFollowing consultations and agreement with the Bank, notify the Government of the incident or accident, within [xx] days, unless otherwise agreed to with the Bank.\n\n \n \nd) Upon request, share Contractors’ and Implementing Partners’ notifications and reports to the Bank within 7 days following the Bank’s\n request, unless otherwise agreed to with the Bank.\n\n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n-- \nElizabeth Duby | Legal Advisor | IPAS | HQ | Copenhagen | Mob:\n+45 30177661  | IPAS\nSolution Portal  | www.unops.org |\n\n \n\nKeep up-to-date with UNOPS. Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube Subscribe to our\nexternal newsletter in English, French or Spanish",
