>;\n ZSOLT DE SOUSA Hugo (EEAS-LIBREVILLE) <Hugo.ZSOLT-DE-SOUSA@eeas.europa.eu>;\n FEIGE Thomas (EEAS-KHARTOUM) <Thomas.FEIGE@eeas.europa.eu>;\n GAVILAN MARIN Jesus (EEAS-KAMPALA) <Jesus-Manuel.GAVILAN-MARIN@eeas.europa.eu>;\n DE RUYT Virginie (INTPA) <Virginie.DE-RUYT@ec.europa.eu>;\n STALMANS Marc (INTPA) <Marc.STALMANS@ec.europa.eu>; NAVARRO\n Lluis (INTPA) <Lluis.NAVARRO@ec.europa.eu>; MURAILLE Berenice\n (INTPA) <Berenice.MURAILLE@ec.europa.eu>; OLIETE JOSA Sergio\n (INTPA) <Sergio.OLIETE-JOSA@ec.europa.eu>; ROFI Daniela\n (INTPA) <Daniela.ROFI@ec.europa.eu>; BOELEN Martijn (INTPA) <Martijn.BOELEN@ec.europa.eu>;\n RUFFATO Chiara (INTPA) <Chiara.RUFFATO@ec.europa.eu>; RUIZ\n SAN JOSE Macarena (INTPA) <Macarena.RUIZ-SAN-JOSE@ec.europa.eu>;\n VERSE Didier (INTPA) <Didier.VERSE@ec.europa.eu>; GRAPSAS Georgios\n (INTPA) <Georgios.GRAPSAS@ec.europa.eu>; DURA George (INTPA)\n <George.DURA@ec.europa.eu>; GEORGELIN Lenaic (INTPA) <Lenaic.GEORGELIN@ec.europa.eu>\n\nCc: MALONGO Jean-Claude (EEAS-NDJAMENA) <Jean-Claude.MALONGO@eeas.europa.eu>;\n CIRES ALONSO Adolfo (EEAS-NAIROBI) <Adolfo.CIRES-ALONSO@eeas.europa.eu>;\n ELSAYED Maisa (EEAS-KHARTOUM) <Maisa.ELSAYED@eeas.europa.eu>;\n VILLEMAGNE-CROS Carine (INTPA) <Carine.VILLEMAGNE-CROS@ec.europa.eu>;\n LEMOINE Francis (INTPA) <Francis.LEMOINE@ec.europa.eu>; DEKEYSER\n Sacha (INTPA) <Sacha.DEKEYSER@ec.europa.eu>; HEYVAERT Valerie\n Catherine (INTPA) <Valerie-Catherine.HEYVAERT@ec.europa.eu>;\n SZILAGYI-KISS Szandra (INTPA) <Szandra.SZILAGYI-KISS@ec.europa.eu>;\n HANO Agnes (INTPA) <Agnes.HANO@ec.europa.eu>; HURJUI Madalina (INTPA)\n <Madalina.HURJUI@ec.europa.eu>; BERNHARD Philippe (INTPA)\n <Philippe.BERNHARD@ec.europa.eu>; ANTONESCU Alexandru (INTPA)\n <Alexandru.ANTONESCU@ec.europa.eu>; LOPEZ VILLAR Sonia\n (INTPA) <Sonia.LOPEZ-VILLAR@ec.europa.eu>; HUNAULT Claire\n (INTPA) <Claire.HUNAULT@ec.europa.eu>; FOX Stephan (EEAS-NAIROBI)\n <Stephan.FOX@eeas.europa.eu>; CAPONE Antonio (EEAS-BUJUMBURA)\n <Antonio.CAPONE@eeas.europa.eu>; JACQUES DE DIXMUDE Arnold\n (EEAS-BUJUMBURA) <Arnold.JACQUES-DE-DIXMUDE@eeas.europa.eu>;\n HAJI Mohamed (EEAS-NAIROBI) <Mohamed.HAJI@eeas.europa.eu>\n\nSubject: Central Africa coordination & pipeline – Take aways meeting 6th December\n\n\n \nDear colleagues,\n\n \nMany thanks for your active participation on 6/12 in this first subregional coordination meeting. The main objective of the meeting was to ensure a better coordination at subregional level and\n to set up a system that reinforces the way of working in a more coherent way, connecting the different levels of intervention (corridor- regional- continental).\nAttached you can find the PPT that was presented. We note that there were some elements missing and to be corrected, we will work on it for the next session.\n \nPlease see below some takeaways and comments, let us know if there is anything missing that needs to be urgently addressed.\n\n \nMain takeaways:\n\n-         \nImportant to have this sub-regional vision to share experiences form corridor to corridor and to ensure that budgetary commitments under the Eastern-Central Africa budget lines are respected. Suggest to organize\n this regional coordination meeting on a monthly basis (recurrent invitation to be shared by HQ).\n\n\n-         \nMeetings for coordination at corridor level to continue in parallel, led by lead EUD. Exchanges with HQ per corridor will also continue in parallel to continue finetuning proposals.\n\n\n-         \nImportant to define a narrative/ logic of intervention along the corridors that guide the proposed interventions per corridor -\n(corridor approach justification).\n\n-         \nLead Delegations role welcomed by participants, lead EUD to ensure the coordination and gathering of input for definition of corridor approach and for the submission of proposals to HQ.\n\n\n-         \nProposals for action along the corridors can be shaped as standalone individual projects contributing to the defined corridor logic\n(ie. Port of Dar er Salam proposal) or shaped under a unified proposal to cover the overall corridor (ie. corridor Douala- Kampala).\nContracting authority can be different from the lead EUD. \n\n-         \nHQ to develop and share (January 2024) a document/file that could allow monitoring all updates and proposals in a more automatic way, as well as to share info on what is being done (access to all EUDs).\n\n-         \nImportant to look at all support measures in a more integrated manner. HQ to further reflect on a system that allows to see all (soft and hard) proposals in a comprehensive way.\n\n-         \nHQ to set up a decision system (with geographic and thematic units) that will allow joint decision making with a broader subregional vision linked to the budget lines. Tentative calendar find below.\n\n-         \nTo have a more regular system and make sure that contracting is done within N+1, we would suggest to have 2 periods in which proposals can be submitted and reviewed within the broader vision.\n\n \nCorridor by corridor observations:\n\n-         \nCorridor 5, lead Delegation is finetuning unified proposal along the corridor. More developed concept note will be shared for comments and feedback. Good to have indicative contracting foreseen.\n\n-         \nCorridor 6, lead Delegation has sent already the proposed approach. Overall very clear and we now expect a more developed concept with indicative contracting timeline for comments and feedback\n\n-         \nCorridor 7, *\nExceptionally and considering the urgency in terms of timing, feedback and way forward will be shared for the Dar er Salam proposal (corridor 7) before end 2024.\nIn parallel, lead Delegation with National EUDs to work in the development of the corridor logic (including the port proposal) and assess the relevance of other interventions proposed along the corridor.\n\n\n-         \nCorridor 8, lead Delegation finetuning unified proposal and working in consolidating different country proposals. More developed concept note will be shared for comments and feedback.\n*On the “Joint border post proposal” to confirm whether this would fit in the proposal that will be submitted or requires urgent ad-hoc discussion (similar to Dar er Salam), indicate timeline.\n\n\n-         \nCorridor 11, lead Delegation to finetune the proposal and confirm whether this would be the full intervention in the corridor, together with overall corridor logic approach for intervention.\n \nProposed calendar -\nfocused on budget N+1 2024 – 31.3 M€\n \n\n\n\n\nBy 20th January\n\n\n\nLead Delegations to share brief paragraph with the intended approach and narrative guiding the work in the corridor\n(logic of intervention and focus of action). \n\n\n\n\nBy 15th March \n\n\nLead EUDs to share mature proposals (concept note/s) to be contracted before the end of the year 2024\n(1st round)\n\n\n\n\nBy 1st April\n\n\nHQ (A4, E2, F4, A2) to send feedback and way forward for contracting on the corridor proposals\n\n\n\n\n\nBy 15th May\n\n\nLead EUDs to share mature proposals (concept note/s) to be contracted before the end of the year 2024\n(2nd round) and if ready also for 2025\n\n\n\n\nBy 1st June\n\n\nHQ (A4, E2, F4, A2) to send feedback and way forward for contracting on the corridor proposals\n\n\n\n\n \n \nFabio, Macarena, Georgios\n \n\n-----Original Appointment-----\n\nFrom: DI STEFANO Fabio (INTPA) \n\nSent: Thursday, November 30, 2023 12:42 PM\nDear colleagues in the East and Central Africa regions,\n\n \nAs the work on the  strategic corridors advances, A2, A4, F4 and E2  would like to have a meeting with all of you, Eastern-Central African colleagues concerned by the corridors, to provide an\n overview and assess the proposals put forward under this sub-region. This general overview is particularly important in view of the annual commitments under each budget line and to strengthen internal coherence.\n\n \nWe would suggest to have 2 main points of discussion:\n \n\n-         \nGeneral overview: State of play of the different actions in support of the strategic corridors and ways of working\n\no   \nTransport infrastructure – blending  (AAP2021)\n\no   \nSoft Measures Trade and transport facilitation (AAP 2023) –  Commission decision to be adopted next week\n\no   \nRoad Governance – (AAP 2023)\n\no   \nNew potential actions fragility – (future AAPs)\n\n-         \nDetailed presentation and discussion of proposals in the pipeline to be contracted in 2024 -\nWith particular focus on soft trade and transport facilitation action (N+1)\n\n-         \nAOB\n \nWe would like to call a meeting  on Thursday 30th   November.\n \nHope this works for all.\n\n \nMany thanks for the constant work and engagement on this matter.\n\n \nKind regards, \n\nDaniela Rofi and Didier Verse",
