                new (
                    "00TQx00000JQcwOMAT",
                    new UNOPSInteraction
                    {
                        Name = "Email: Fwd: WB SFAs: Do not use new template posted on website on 13 November",
                        Type = InteractionType.Email,
                        Date = DateTime.Parse("2024-12-18").ToUniversalTime(),
                        Subject = "Email: Fwd: WB SFAs: Do not use new template posted on website on 13 November",
                        Description = "To: partnerships.focal.points@unops.org; leyres@unops.org; AkiraM@unops.org; basazinewt@unops.org\nCC: emiliep@unops.org; freyavg@unops.org; elizabethdu@unops.org; christinebo@unops.org\nBCC: \nAttachment: --none--\n\nSubject: Fwd: WB SFAs: Do not use new template posted on website on 13 November\nBody:\nDear colleagues,\nI hope you are well. No agreement has yet been reached on the new SFA with the World Bank, although a number of discussions in recent days have produced potential solutions to the remaining issues. \nIn the meantime, we have been able to continue signing the \"interim solution\" on a project-by-project basis for engagements under an advanced stage of preparation. The World Bank has asked WLO to share an updated list of these projects, and we plan to provide as comprehensive an update as possible to them this Friday 20 December. To that end, and following on from Christine's email below, please update the urgent cases spreadsheet with the latest available information on your projects. You can add the info in rows 74-76 (and feel free to add more rows in that part of the sheet as needed). Columns J to L will remain for internal use only and will not be shared with the Bank. \nThank you for your attention, as this is an important chance to smooth the path toward project signature for projects going into the new year. \nStay tuned in the Partnerships Network and over email in the coming days for our 2024 wrap-up from Washington on the World Bank and other partnerships activities here in DC. Thanks for all your support and collaboration on this collective effort, which we are continuing to push forward with colleagues across the organisation going into the new year. \nBest wishes for the holiday season, \nAlistair \n\n---------- Forwarded message ---------\nFrom: Christine BOWERS <christinebo@unops.org>\nDate: Wed, Dec 11, 2024 at 11:46 AM\nSubject: Fwd: WB SFAs: Do not use new template posted on website on 13 November\nTo: Amy NIANG <amyn@unops.org>, Elena GEORGALLA <elenage@unops.org>, Simonetta SILIGATO <simonettas@unops.org>, Marija BATEMAN <marijab@unops.org>, Sophie DJUGELI <sophiedj@unops.org>, Robert GODIN <robertgodin@unops.org>, Sol VILLA MICHEL MORALES <solv@unops.org>\nCc: Kirstine DAMKJAER <kirstined@unops.org>, Sonja LEIGHTON-KONE <sonjalk@unops.org>, Bana KALOTI <banak@unops.org>, Tim LARDNER <timl@unops.org>, Sanjay MATHUR <sanjaym@unops.org>, Fabrizio FELICIANI <fabriziof@unops.org>, Emilie POTVIN <emiliep@unops.org>, Dalila Alexandra RAMOS MOREIRA GONCALVES <dalilag@unops.org>, Nicole JORDAN <nicolej@unops.org>, Freya von GROOTE <freyavg@unops.org>, Tanja CHOPRA <tanjac@unops.org>, Julia SCHIPPER <juliasc@unops.org>, Nives COSTA <nivesc@unops.org>, Elizabeth Agathe DUBY <elizabethdu@unops.org>, Worknesh Mekonnen GONET <workneshg@unops.org>, Sonja VARGA <sonjav@unops.org>, Elene AGLADZE <eleneag@unops.org>, Basazinew TEREFE <basazinewt@unops.org>, Sadia SAEED <sadias@unops.org>, Francesca PANCIERA <francescap@unops.org>, Ala'a NEMER <alaan@unops.org>, Muhammad Asim Ayub <muhammada@unops.org>, Akira MORETTO <AkiraM@unops.org>, Leslie Mhara <lesliemh@unops.org>, Alice Norindr <alicen@unops.org>, Dabagai DABAGAI <dabagaid@unops.org>, Jakob TUBORGH <jakobt@unops.org>, Reem ASKAR <reemas@unops.org>, Leyre SASTRE SEBASTIAN <leyres@unops.org>, Simon PORTER <simonp@unops.org>, Abdoul Aziz SOUMARE <abdoulazizs@unops.org>, Tokumitsu KOBAYASHI <tokumitsuk@unops.org>, Joeena SIMPSON <joeenas@unops.org>, Sirpa Helena JARVENPAA <sirpaj@unops.org>, Petronella Kasaka HALWIINDI <petronellah@unops.org>, Fumie ARIMIZU <fumiea@unops.org>, <plg.personnel@unops.org>, Itai MUKUVARI <itaim@unops.org>, Lwanda Kafumukache KAHONGO <lwandak@unops.org>, Clara GOMEZ HERNANDEZ <claragh@unops.org>, Seynabou DIENG <seynaboud@unops.org>, Lydia ATIENO OPIYO <lydiaat@unops.org>, Eloise Virginie LE MAGNEN <eloiselm@unops.org>, Fatou NDIAYE <fatoufn@unops.org>, Claudia ROSANO <claudiar@unops.org>, Sven ECKERT <svene@unops.org>, Sofia Teresa MELENDEZ APARICIO <sofiateresama@unops.org>, Alistair Peter SOMERVILLE <alistairs@unops.org>\n\nDear colleagues,\nThe below advice continues to apply to World Bank projects under preparation. Discussions with the World Bank and with other UN agencies on the new template for Outputs and Technical Assistance continue, with deep appreciation to the many colleagues who have contributed to this effort in recent months. We will not be able to agree a new template until January at the soonest.\nWLO again met with WB HQ today to discuss pending cases and the broader negotiation. We have been asked for a list of all projects to which the interim solution (i.e. old SFA updated with new ESS language) should apply, which we will share with the WB on Dec 18. Mindful that there may be further delays in agreeing a new template, I ask you to please update the urgent cases spreadsheet with available information on projects with signature expected between now and March using the Outputs or TA SFA. If you would like to share information on projects that are awaiting the new template (because they will be signed much later), will use the Supplies or Vehicles SFA, or with an amendment in process (which shouldn't be affected), you are also welcome to do so, as there are columns for you to flag this information.\nAs it may assist you in discussions at field level, please note that UNICEF, WFP, FAO and IOM, like UNOPS, have not signed any projects using the November template. I am unaware of any UN agencies which have used the template. (UNICEF has agreed and used a new standard template with WB for direct finance but they do not agree to the indirect finance template on the WB website.)\nPlease continue to rely on guidance from LG on the appropriate template and specific questions on contractual language, and consider tagging me in relevant tickets. You should also continue to reach out to WLO (myself and Alistair) with questions, particularly if your WB or government counterparts are insisting on the new template. If you would like us to advocate with WB HQ on your  behalf, please share an email which we can forward on to WB, containing basic information on context, the TTL's name and ideally the WB project number. We have been able to resolve several cases this way.\nWarm regardsChristine\n---------- Forwarded message ---------\nFrom: Christine BOWERS <christinebo@unops.org>\nDate: Thu, Nov 14, 2024 at 4:31 PM\nSubject: WB SFAs: Do not use new template posted on website on 13 November\nTo: Amy NIANG <amyn@unops.org>, Elena GEORGALLA <elenage@unops.org>, Simonetta SILIGATO <simonettas@unops.org>, Marija BATEMAN <marijab@unops.org>, Sophie DJUGELI <sophiedj@unops.org>, Robert GODIN <robertgodin@unops.org>, Sol VILLA MICHEL MORALES <solv@unops.org>\nCc: Kirstine DAMKJAER <kirstined@unops.org>, Bana KALOTI <banak@unops.org>, Tim LARDNER <timl@unops.org>, Sanjay MATHUR <sanjaym@unops.org>, Fabrizio FELICIANI <fabriziof@unops.org>, Emilie POTVIN <emiliep@unops.org>, Dalila Alexandra RAMOS MOREIRA GONCALVES <dalilag@unops.org>, Nicole JORDAN <nicolej@unops.org>, Tanja CHOPRA <tanjac@unops.org>, Julia SCHIPPER <juliasc@unops.org>, Nives COSTA <nivesc@unops.org>, Vinesh WINODAN <vineshw@unops.org>, Elizabeth Agathe DUBY <elizabethdu@unops.org>, Worknesh Mekonnen GONET <workneshg@unops.org>, Sonja VARGA <sonjav@unops.org>, Elene AGLADZE <eleneag@unops.org>, Basazinew TEREFE <basazinewt@unops.org>, Sadia SAEED <sadias@unops.org>, Francesca PANCIERA <francescap@unops.org>, Ala'a NEMER <alaan@unops.org>, Muhammad Asim Ayub <muhammada@unops.org>, Akira MORETTO <AkiraM@unops.org>, Leslie Mhara <lesliemh@unops.org>, Alice Norindr <alicen@unops.org>, Dabagai DABAGAI <dabagaid@unops.org>, Jakob TUBORGH <jakobt@unops.org>, Reem ASKAR <reemas@unops.org>, Leyre SASTRE SEBASTIAN <leyres@unops.org>, Simon PORTER <simonp@unops.org>, Abdoul Aziz SOUMARE <abdoulazizs@unops.org>, Tokumitsu KOBAYASHI <tokumitsuk@unops.org>, Joeena SIMPSON <joeenas@unops.org>, Sirpa Helena JARVENPAA <sirpaj@unops.org>, Petronella Kasaka HALWIINDI <petronellah@unops.org>, Fumie ARIMIZU <fumiea@unops.org>, Freya von GROOTE <freyavg@unops.org>, Alistair Peter SOMERVILLE <alistairs@unops.org>, <plg.personnel@unops.org>, Itai MUKUVARI <itaim@unops.org>, Lwanda Kafumukache KAHONGO <lwandak@unops.org>, Kelley SWIFT <kelleys@unops.org>, Anton KOHUT <antonk@unops.org>, Clara GOMEZ HERNANDEZ <claragh@unops.org>, Seynabou DIENG <seynaboud@unops.org>\n\nDear colleagues,\nI trust you are well. Following the conclusion of its negotiations with UNICEF, the World Bank posted an updated SFA for Outputs on its website yesterday for use with UN agencies, superseding the July version. A Technical Assistance SFA template will follow, mirroring this version. \nDespite many productive conversations in recent months, there remain a handful of outstanding issues we need to clear internally and with the Bank. Thus, UNOPS is not yet in a position to use this new template. The full text was not shared with UNOPS (or other UN agencies) before publication, and it now requires internal consultations on the final provisions. Please do not use it for any engagements under negotiation.\nWLO, FG, LG, IAIG, and numerous technical specialists are working on internal approvals and reviewing processes to ensure we are able to sign WB agreements and comply with the updated requirements. For those outputs-based agreements currently under negotiation with the WB at an advanced stage, you should plan to continue with the interim solution. Thank you to everyone for the hard work, collaboration, and flexibility on this.\nContinue to use the urgent cases spreadsheet to track updates. Please keep WLO (Christine and Alistair) in the loop with any questions, especially if you receive push-back from WB or government counterparts on use of the interim solution.\nBest regards,Christine\n\nOn Tue, Oct 8, 2024 at 4:42 PM Christine BOWERS <christinebo@unops.org> wrote:\nDear Regional Partnerships Advisors and colleagues,\nI hope you are well, and it has been great to see many of you during the GLN. Today, WLO and LG attended a meeting convened by the World Bank with UN agencies to discuss the latest developments regarding the ongoing SFA negotiations. \n\nWorld Bank senior management set a deadline of 1st November for the Bank to conclude standard Output and Technical Assistance templates applicable to all UN agencies (with minor variation within the templates per agency as needed). However, we view it as unlikely that negotiations will be concluded by 1st November. \nThe Bank’s final negotiations with UNICEF have not yet concluded, and neither the Bank nor UNICEF has shared the full text of the near-final Output SFA, reflecting the various changes introduced to the July 2024 version. While we have discussed extensively the Environmental and Social obligations with WB colleagues, UNOPS has not had the opportunity to negotiate important adjustments related to project finance and reporting. More than a dozen UN agencies are having bilateral conversations with the WB on the template.\nWe learned today that should negotiations not be concluded by 1st November, and no new template agreed by that date, there will be a pause on our ability to sign new Bank-financed projects. The “interim solution” currently in use will no longer apply after the end of October. At today’s meeting I was joined by other UN colleagues in expressing concern about a complete pause in signing engagements, however the WB colleagues (led by Chief Counsel of Environmental and International Law) were firm.\nI’ve updated the table of potentially affected projects - feel free to make additions and corrections or send an email update. \nIf you are experiencing challenges with WB TTLs in implementing the interim solution, please let me know ASAP (with Alistair Somerville in copy). We can have WB HQ reach out to the TTL directly to ensure they are updated with the latest guidance. For fastest resolution, send us a brief email on the project status and the specific issue you are facing with WB colleagues, including contract type (Output or TA), contract value, WB TTL name and ideally WB project number. We will then forward your message to relevant WB focal points for help.\nPlease do not hesitate to send an email to myself with Alistair Somerville (alistairs@unops.org) in copy with any questions. We are continuing to seek further details from WB, and LG continues its excellent support in consolidating our negotiating positions.\nSee below messages for additional guidance and resources.\nBest regards,\nChristine\n\nOn Mon, Sep 30, 2024 at 6:03 PM Christine BOWERS <christinebo@unops.org> wrote:\nDear colleagues,\nIn case you missed it last week, please find here the slides and recording for the Partnership Sparks session where I and Vinesh provided an update on the status of negotiations with the World Bank (and our upgraded legal commitments), and where Tanja provided an overview of the World Bank's Environmental and Social Framework and how it impacts UNOPS project implementation.\nTanja Chopra has kindly agreed to follow this overall introduction to the ESF with a 90-minute session with each region, where you can raise your specific questions and go deeper. I'd advise each regional partnerships advisor to reach out directly to Tanja to schedule a session. Please also ensure that your teams have watched the above Sparks session in advance, so that you will have more time for knowledgeable questions.\nThe interim versions of the Outputs and Technical Assistance Standard Forms of Agreement were updated last week; please continue using these for new engagements which will be signed in the coming weeks. As a reminder, the old versions of Supplies and Vehicles templates should be used; they remain unchanged. Do not use any other templates which you might receive from WB or government counterparts.\nThe World Bank has finally concluded negotiations on a final template with UNICEF, and they will be turning to us next. If you have comments on the SFA that you would like to be considered as we negotiate, please share these with me and Elizabeth Duby (who has been consolidating comments on the template). The WB is still working towards a November 1st deadline to have final templates for Outputs and TA with UN agencies. We will also negotiate a direct finance template, building on the versions recently agreed.\nPlease continue using the engagement tickets to communicate with LG for sign-off on your agreements and for any help needed in modifying language. Please reach out to Tanja for support on interpreting specific ESS requirements. Please reach out to me if you need further support or guidance, particularly as it relates to getting updated information to your WB counterparts.\nThanks to all of you for your patience as we continue working with WB focal points to resolve these issues.\nWarm regardsChristine\nOn Wed, Sep 4, 2024 at 3:42 PM Christine BOWERS <christinebo@unops.org> wrote:\nDear All, \nThis message provides an important update on the templates used when UNOPS receives indirect financing from the Work Bank through a Standard Form of Agreement for Outputs or Technical Assistance.  \nInterim Template Usage: We are pleased to share with you the Interim Outputs SFA  and the Interim Technical Assistance SFA for your immediate use. This is the result of close collaboration between the World Bank, LG and WLO. Note that the Vehicles and Supplies SFA templates remain unchanged; the old versions should be used.\nUpdated ESS Clause: The ESS language has been revised and approved on an interim basis. These are the previously used templates, now updated with the newly negotiated Environmental and Social Safeguards. Important: Please pay attention to the “Note to Users” under Clause 23 (Environmental and Social Risk Management).  It is important that these guidelines are followed.  If you have any questions, please request support from the LG reviewer in charge of the engagement review. \nEffective Timing: These interim templates can be used from now until we agree a final updated SFA template with the World Bank (likely to be in late October). Any updates to the templates in the meantime will be circulated to Regional Partnership Advisors, LG reviewers and other relevant colleagues. \nUrgent Projects Using the New Template: Any engagement at an advanced stage of negotiation, including but not only those listed in the control sheet, can use the new template. Please follow the steps outlined by Vinesh’s email of 19 August related to risk assessment. Following a request from WB HQ, regional partnership advisors (and others), please also update the control sheet with additional projects you expect to sign using the new template in September or October. If you need help managing relationships or sharing information with your World Bank counterparts, please contact the Washington Liaison Office for support.\nWorld Bank Internal Guidance: Due to the vacation schedules of several key World Bank colleagues, there has been limited information distributed centrally from Washington to World Bank field teams. We understand that an internal guidance note on the interim template usage (for all UN partners) will be distributed in the next few days to World Bank regional teams, to follow up a brief message which was shared already to World Bank country lawyers. We continue to seek a formal communication from the World Bank which UNOPS teams would be able to share with World Bank and government counterparts to reduce confusion. \nThe situation remains fluid, and we will keep you updated. We appreciate your patience as we continue working with urgent projects while negotiating the permanent template. Look out for an upcoming announcement of the Sparks session on the World Bank’s Environment and Social Framework, which will occur on 26 September at 15:00 CPH.\nPlease don’t hesitate to reach out with any updates, questions or concerns.\nWarm regards,Christine, Vinesh and Elizabeth\n\nOn Mon, Aug 26, 2024 at 10:01 PM Christine BOWERS <christinebo@unops.org> wrote:\nDear colleagues,\n\nGood news. After a series of internal discussions, the World Bank has just now agreed to implement the interim solution as described in my email below. In summary, the old agreement templates for Outputs and Technical Assistance will be retrofitted with the new obligations connected to the Environmental and Social Standards (ESS), while negotiations continue on permanent new templates. \nPrecise wording of the updated ESS clauses is being fine tuned this week. Once we have updated, interim SFA templates we will circulate them via the regional partnership advisors.\nThe guidance on conducting a project-specific assessment of the ESS-related risks remains applicable (see email from Vinesh dated August 19). Please continue to seek guidance from LG via the engagement tickets for support in finalizing new agreements, whether or not they were listed as being approved in the control sheet.\nWhile the enhanced ESS obligations in new World Bank templates are not negotiable, we are anticipating some minor changes to the rest of the new Standard Forms of Agreement (SFA) to be negotiated in the next several weeks. WLO and LG, along with relevant colleagues, will prepare a decision memo for UNOPS management, including information on the implications of the new SFA and a recommended set of internal improvements to balance the risks and opportunities posed by the enhanced ESS obligations.\nPlease contact me, Elizabeth Duby or Vinesh Winodan with any questions. PLG will organize a Sparks session on the World Bank's Environmental and Social Framework in September with LG and Tanja Chopra, in addition to the regional outreach mentioned below.\nWarm regardsChristine\nOn Wed, Aug 14, 2024, 8:22 AM Christine BOWERS <christinebo@unops.org> wrote:\nDear Regional Directors and Regional Partnerships Advisors,Thank you for your continued support in managing the situation following the introduction of an updated Standard Form of Agreements (SFA) by the World Bank. Please find below an update on the new requirements for UNOPS in respect to the WB Environmental and Social Framework (ESF), which I request you to carefully review. (RDs, please pay particular attention to the section on UNOPS responsibilities and associated risks.)If desired, WLO and the Technical Services Unit (Kenya MCO) would be happy to organize a 2-hour presentation on the World Bank’s Environmental and Social Framework (ESF) and discuss any bottlenecks to implementation in your region. Background: The World Bank recently posted on its website an updated framework agreement for use with UN agencies, effective 12 July 2024. Sections of this new template were not agreed/negotiated in advance with UNOPS or other UN agencies. The Bank took this unilateral action in response to urgent internal pressures on compliance with its Environmental and Social Framework (ESF) by UN agencies. UNOPS is in touch with other UN agencies on the specific language being agreed, aiming for a coordinated response where possible.While the underlying requirements of the Environmental and Social Framework have not changed, and we have largely been implementing the requested measures since they were introduced in 2018, the legal templates are being updated to more clearly outline UNOPS’ responsibilities. As communicated earlier by WLO, UNOPS has not cleared the new SFA Output and Technical Assistance templates introduced by the Bank, as our HQ-level negotiations are still ongoing. In the meantime, the WB’s Operations Policy & Country Services and UNOPS are nearing agreement on a set of interim measures to minimize the disruption to projects nearing signature.Affected Projects: This message applies to projects that are signed between UNOPS and government partners for World Bank-financed activities using the Outputs or Technical Assistance templates. We are seeking clarification on how minor amendments to existing projects will be treated.Projects using the Vehicles or Supply templates are not affected, as these agreement templates haven’t changed and remain valid. Similarly, projects receiving direct financing from the World Bank are not affected, as these agreements are individually negotiated without a template.Anticipated Interim Measures: Urgent cases—specifically, those projects listed as “approved” in the control sheet– will use the pre-existing Output or Technical Assistance templates, with the new Environmental and Social Standards (ESS) legal language incorporated. See here the current negotiation status of the language, noting that this remains draft language.For these projects, project-level WB counterparts will also be informed of these exceptional measures once finalized. These projects are expected to be signed by 31 August.Once we reach agreement with the World Bank on interim language, regional partnership advisors and focal points for affected projects will receive an email from WLO confirming the interim measures. This email will be for you to share with World Bank or government partners as needed. (The present email is for internal use.) We expect to activate the interim measures within the next day or two, depending on internal WB processes.UNOPS responsibilities and associated risks: The updated ESS provisions in the SFA require UNOPS to ensure our own compliance with the Environmental and Social Commitment Plan of projects (or similar document), as well as cascade related ESS obligations onto subcontractors and consultants via our contracts with them. We agree to ensure the capacity of these third parties to meet their ESS obligations and to monitor their compliance (and require remedial actions as needed).The newly added language on UNOPS responsibilities introduces additional legal risk exposure to UNOPS, because if there is any loss or damage to third parties, arising partly or wholly due to any failure by UNOPS to perform its ESS obligations, then UNOPS may be held responsible under the SFA to repay those losses or damages to affected third parties. \nNote also that the World Bank is requiring UNOPS to share some of our (redacted) contracts to support verification of the above. We are proposing that we receive these requests from the Bank, rather than the government, to minimize frivolous requests and reduce administrative burdens. This point is still under negotiation.\nGuidance to Engagement DOA holders: Colleagues are advised to review carefully the E&S obligations contained in Annex 1 of new project agreements, with the advice of technical experts as needed, in the context of their specific engagements. Tanja Chopra in the Technical Services Unit at the Kenya MCO, has a small team which can support you with interpreting (or revising) ESS project documents if desired.\nYou should confirm that UNOPS has the capacity and resources to comply with those additional responsibilities before accepting the engagement. If considerable additional expertise or manpower is needed, then you may need to seek additional resources to be included in the budget. \nEngagement DOA holders are also advised to assess the additional risks that may arise from the updated E&S clauses, and identify whether any additional risk mitigation measures are necessary in the context of their specific engagements. The additional risks may be nil to minimal for engagements where there are no ESS considerations, and they may also be so considerable that it will result in the engagement no longer being acceptable to UNOPS. Next steps: As noted above, once the interim language is agreed WLO will communicate with regional partnerships advisors and affected project focal points. It will be several weeks until corporate negotiations conclude on a permanent update to the SFA templates, thus WLO will work with OPCS on a process to support projects with expected signature in September and October. WLO will continue to work through regional partnership advisors to request information and share updates.As Megumi Uchino completes her mission to the Washington Liaison Office on August 23, colleagues should direct project-level updates and requests for inclusion in the next round of interim measures to Christine Bowers. Deep appreciation goes to Vinesh Winodan, Elizabeth Duby, Megumi Uchino and Alistair Somerville for their excellent and collaborative work on this in the past weeks during my summer leave. Moving forward, I am also available for any general or specific questions related to the above. Note also that your LG colleagues are updated and will be ready to assist via the engagement tickets.I will provide a brief update once the World Bank agrees interim language for immediate use. Also I will be in touch with regional partnerships advisors about scheduling a brief session for each region with Tanja in the coming weeks.Best regards, Christine\n\n-- \nChristine BOWERS | Head of the Washington Liaison Office | 1775 K Street NW, Washington, DC 20006 | Tel: +1 202 428 9694 | www.unops.org Keep up-to-date with UNOPS. Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube. \nSubscribe to our external newsletter in English, French or Spanish. \n\n\n\n\n-- \nChristine BOWERS | Head of the Washington Liaison Office | 1775 K Street NW, Washington, DC 20006 | Tel: +1 202 428 9694 | www.unops.org Keep up-to-date with UNOPS. Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube. \nSubscribe to our external newsletter in English, French or Spanish.",
