                new (
                    "00TQx00000JRzT9MAL",
                    new UNOPSInteraction
                    {
                        Name = "Email: Notes from meeting with Norway’s Embassy in Ethiopia on 12 December, 2025",
                        Type = InteractionType.Email,
                        Date = DateTime.Parse("2024-12-19").ToUniversalTime(),
                        Subject = "Email: Notes from meeting with Norway’s Embassy in Ethiopia on 12 December, 2025",
                        Description = "To: dalilag@unops.org; workneshg@unops.org; petarj@unops.org; elenage@unops.org; amyn@unops.org; mariatew@unops.org\nCC: kirstined@unops.org; emiliep@unops.org; freyavg@unops.org; asbjornb@unops.org; silkeh@unops.org\nBCC: \nAttachment: --none--\n\nSubject: Notes from meeting with Norway’s Embassy in Ethiopia on 12 December, 2025\nBody:\nDear colleagues, \nOn Friday, December 13, Asbjørn, Silke, and I met Astrid Lervåg, Head of Development Cooperation at Norway’s Embassy in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia. Lervåg clarified early on that no funds were currently available.\nAsbjørn provided an overview of UNOPS, capacities, portfolio and key partnerships, including the World Bank, and addressed the comprehensive reforms undertaken by UNOPS since S3i. Silke presented UNOPS’ portfolio and ongoing work in eastern Africa - including a recent visit to Tigray. Lervåg emphasized the need for the population in Tigray to know and understand that the aid was coming from the central government in Addis Ababa to foster peaceful coexistence. \nLervåg showed interest in UNOPS’ gender-based-violence (GBV) work in Tigray, indicating potential funding as the embassy felt that they had not done enough in this area. She also pointed towards Norway's ongoing support to climate adaptation/mitigation and forest preservation, carbon credits, food security, education, and human rights. Ethiopia would remain a key partner for Norway. Silke agreed to provide more information in writing ahead of a follow-up meeting that would involve the Norwegian embassy’s GBV and climate staff.  \nNoting that UNOPS did not have a direct contract with Norway, Lervåg informed that Norway had provided funding via KfW which entailed the procurement of 90 vehicles - of which 30 was funded by Norway. KfW had used UNOPS for procurement and the money seemed to have disappeared without any vehicles having been delivered. Silke and Asbjørn assured Lervåg that they would follow up on the situation to clarify what had happened.\nFollow-up actions: Silke  will provide more information on UNOPS’ efforts in combating GBVSilke will provide a clarification of what happened with the procurement of vehicles on behalf of KfW. Silke committed to sharing further details regarding UNOPS’ initiatives to mitigate carbon emissions.Silke will share an overview of impact figures for the work done in Tigray and as a well as a follow-up meeting in Q1                                                                            ***On Friday December 13, Asbjørn, Silke and I met with Astrid Lervåg, Head of Development Cooperation at Norway’s embassy in Ethiopia. \nLervåg initially noted that the embassy currently had no available funding. Responding to this, Asbjørn made it clear that the intention of the meeting was merely to introduce UNOPS’ portfolio of work in Ethiopia and making the embassy aware of UNOPS’ capacities. \nAsbjørn started the meeting by providing an overview of UNOPS, noting that the World Bank is UNOPS’ biggest partner. He also addressed the comprehensive reforms of UNOPS following S3i and current positive relations at HQ level between UNOPS and Norway. Silke provided an overview of UNOPS Africa and the Ethiopia Multicountry Office. \nLervåg noted that she had recently attended The Development Partners Group meeting, where UNOPS provided a great presentation on its work in the Tigray region. Lervåg asked if UNOPS could be helpful in providing impact figures for the work done in Tigray, which Silke confirmed. Lervåg also pointed toward the need to ensure that the government of Ethiopia was sufficiently credited for the project to help facilitate the sensitive relationship between the population in Tigray and the federal government in Addis. \nSilke noted that the efforts in remote areas affected by the conflict was important and highly appreciated by the local population, which could be testified by the recent visit. Asbjørn informed that UNOPS’ projects were mainly driven by local colleagues who very well understood the sensitive context and how appreciative and emotional the local community was during the recent handover of one of the schools. Asbjørn further informed that the community also very well appreciated the local contractors as well as stones, woods and equipment used to build the schools.  \nLervåg appreciated the impressions and suggested a common site visit in the future to see the impact of UNOPS’ projects - which Silke confirmed that she would be happy to organise. \nLervåg expressed particular interest in the combating gender-based violence (GBV) component of the Tigray portfolio, acknowledging that the embassy had not yet done enough in this area. She mentioned that GBV was an area where the embassy could be interested in providing funding. In response, Silke promised to follow up with more details on UNOPS’ efforts to combat GBV, particularly highlighting the infrastructure aspect and UNOPS’ capacity to build safehouses.\nNoting that UNOPS does not have a formal contract with Norway, Lervåg informed about an agreement with KfW which entailed the procurement of 90 vehicles - of which 30 was funded by Norway. According to Lervåg, KfW had used UNOPS for procurement and the money unfortunately seemed to have gotten lost. She expressed confidence that KfW would resolve the issue but mentioned that it remains unresolved as of now. Silke and Asbjørn assured that they would look into the matter and follow up asap.    Lervåg informed that after Ukraine, Ethiopia was Norway’s second second-largest partner - with a particular focus on climate and forest. Specific areas of collaboration included:\nCarbon Credit: Supporting Ethiopia’s ambition to establish carbon credit initiatives, with UNDP serving as the procurement partner and FAO as the monitoring partner.Food Security: Working with the government and NGOs to advance climate-smart agriculture.\nLervåg noted that the resilience component in the Tigray portfolio was an interesting opportunity for Norway. Silke promised to follow up with more information on UNOPS’ efforts to reduce carbon emissions. \nLastly, Lervåg highlighted that Norway provided funding within education through the World bank and UNICEF’s education in emergencies, where focus was on capacity building of teachers. Silke and Asbjørn complemented the work done by UNICEF and UNDP and pointed towards the need to also provide infrastructure in the education sector, which UNOPS was uniquely positioned to deliver - which Lervåg took notice of.   \nBest, Naimo\n-- \n\nNaimo Hassan Hirsi | Partnerships Analyst | Partnerships and Liaison Group | UNOPS HQ | Copenhagen, Denmark | Mob: +45 26 83 48 78  | www.unops.org Keep up-to-date with UNOPS. Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube Subscribe to our external newsletter in English, French or Spanish.",
