                new (
                    "00TQx00000KEPTCMA5",
                    new UNOPSInteraction
                    {
                        Name = "Email: Documents for upcoming Executive Board 27-31 January",
                        Type = InteractionType.Email,
                        Date = DateTime.Parse("2025-01-13").ToUniversalTime(),
                        Subject = "Email: Documents for upcoming Executive Board 27-31 January",
                        Description = "To: Agnes.Annells@fcdo.gov.uk; Sally.Clifford@fcdo.gov.uk\nCC: naimoh@unops.org\nBCC: \nAttachment: --none--\n\nSubject: Documents for upcoming Executive Board 27-31 January\nBody:\nDear Agnes and Sally,\nA late - but very happy new year to both of you! I hope you have had a chance to enjoy some offline time with your loved ones. \nAs usual I wanted to provide you with a brief overview of the topics relevant for UNOPS at the upcoming EB session on January 27-31. I am sorry that I am a bit late with this overview, as we already have the first informal session this afternoon at 5 pm CET about the status of the Comprehensive Response Plan and UNOPS' digitalisation plan (the so-called PID). \nI am sure that you have already seen that all the relevant documents were uploaded to the website here - but I just wanted to share the link with you to gather all the pieces in one email. \nI was wondering if perhaps you would have time to briefly catch up in advance of the EB - to get your initial take on the topics and for me to perhaps clarify matters, point towards (for us) important aspects, etc. If so - please just let me know when it might suit you. \nFurther to the topics to be discussed at the upcoming EB, I have provided you with a quick overview of the upcoming informal session below for your ease of reference. 13 January: The comprehensive response plan and the UNOPS report on the Process Innovation and Digitalization Programme implementation 14 January: Informal consultation with UNDP, UNFPA, UNOPS on the joint update on organization culture 17 January: Joint UNDP, UNFPA, UNOPS orientation for the Executive Board (our DED Sonja will give an introductory presentation on UNOPS to the EB members)20 January: Briefing on undisbursed excess reserves, with UNBOA 21 January: Joint consultation on enterprise risk management 22 January: Joint informal with JIU on JIU report on governance and oversight There will also be two closed session during the EB week:28 January: Closed briefing on S3i funds recovery, with OLA30 January: Closed briefing on portfolio risk assessment  Topics to be discussed at the EB relevant to UNOPSUNOPS' response to the recommendations of the UN Board of Auditors for 2023The UNBOA report for 2023 on UNOPS was unqualified (also known as \"clean\") - meaning that our financial statements are deemed to present a true and fair view of UNOPS' financial position and performance, comply with UN accounting standards and are free of material misstatements. UNBOA issued 9 recommendations classified as high priority and 10 recommendations classified as medium priority.  The implementation of all recommendations is on track for completion within the timelines. Because the recommendations were issued in July 2024, and many of them require long-term attention, UNOPS will need to work beyond 2024 to implement them all successfully.In the implementation schedule UNOPS is targeting an implementation rate of 68% during the 2024 audit cycle, which will surpass that of 2023 (58%). \n\nGovernance and oversight functions of the Executive Board At the last EB session the Bureau was tasked to develop a terms of reference for the operationalisation of a working group to review the 31 recommendations of the Joint Inspection Unit (JIU) and to develop actions for their implementation. In advance of the last EB session in 2024, UNOPS developed this informal document outlining UNOPS' views on the JIU recommendations. The document also contains the views of the ethics offices of the involved agencies. \n\nRisk managementAt the last EB session, UNDP, UNFPA and UNOPS were asked to provide updates on the agencies' efforts to improve the enterprise risk management system and inform the EB of any critical risks of strategic importance. From a UNOPS perspective The joint information note points towards key risks associated with project delivery, financial management and stakeholder engagement. It also points to more concrete risks regarding UNOPS' operations in fragile and conflict affected places, shortage of talent, sexual abuse due to the geographical operations of UNOPS, regaining trust among partners since S3i failures, implementing the many reforms at UNOPS and increasing financial constraints.     \n\nOrganisational cultureThe informal information note provides an update on the implementation of the work plan to transform the organisational culture. Status of each action point is in this annex.    \n\nImplementation of the Comprehensive Response PlanThis is the last update to the EB on the implementation of the CRP. As the note was written in November, 3 of the outstanding recommendations were yet to be completed by the end of 2024. These related to 1) revising the management and organisational culture towards broader engagement and less top-down, 2) invest in outcome-reporting, and 3) revision of financial rules and regulations.  The EB is asked to release the third tranche (11.8M USD) for the implementation and to initiate the second independent review of the implementation of the CRP.  \n\nReview of the whistleblower processThe informal information note provides an overview of the now completed overhaul of UNOPS' whistleblower mechanism. The new system is set to launch in January 2025 and will introduce: 1) a central platform for reporting, 2) ressources to provide early informal guidance, 3) a workplace conduct team within PLG, 4) a new advisory group to address systemic issues. \n\nPortfolio risk assessment of UNOPSAs a response to the original KPMG recommendations a portfolio risk assessment is presented that outlines a number of risks in UNOPS' portfolio. These relate e.g. to 1) challenges in managing complex infrastructure projects, 2) large-scale procurement of health products, 3) capacity of supplivers and implementing partners. \n\nCalculation method for excess reservesThe note presents a suggestion for revising the calculation methodology for calculating UNOPS' excess reserves. The proposed changes are to ensure that only liquid assets are calculated as part of the potential excess reserves and that only if the excess reserve exceeds 20% of the minimum operational reserve, it should trigger the refund process. By the end of 2023 UNOPS distributed 123.2M USD of the excess reserve back to partners and all other funds had been transferred to the RC system and the SDG fund. Only 0.6M USD is withheld as these relate to discussions with partners. \n\nImplementation of the PIDThis is the annual status of implementation of the PID that points towards progress in seven programmes (country engagements, partnerships and project management, procurement, HR, finance, data, market research for the new ERP). Notice that the selection of a new ERP system can only be done after a formal evaluation is presented to the EB (as requested by the EB). Therefore the timeline for the PID has been extended by one year to 2028.I hope this brief overview helps - and I look very much forward to hearing your initial thinking about the topics. \nAll the best,Asbjørn-- \n\nAsbjørn Brink| Head of Northern Europe Liaison Office | Partnerships and Liaison Group | HQ | Copenhagen, Denmark | Tel: +45 40 80 36 54 | Follow us on social media and subscribe to our newsletter. | www.unops.org",
