                ),
                new (
                    "00TQx00000LJcpKMAT",
                    new UNOPSInteraction
                    {
                        Name = "Email: Re: Retrofitted Technical Assistance SFA for Engagements at an Advanced Stage of Preparation",
                        Type = InteractionType.Email,
                        Date = DateTime.Parse("2025-02-06").ToUniversalTime(),
                        Subject = "Email: Re: Retrofitted Technical Assistance SFA for Engagements at an Advanced Stage of Preparation",
                        Description = "To: bkuba@worldbank.org\nCC: christinebo@unops.org; alistairs@unops.org; amicheli@worldbank.org; mfikre@worldbank.org; gkassem1@worldbank.org; akircher1@worldbank.org; xli2@worldbank.org\nBCC: \nAttachment: --none--\n\nSubject: Re: Retrofitted Technical Assistance SFA for Engagements at an Advanced Stage of Preparation\nBody:\nHi Birgit, \nI appreciate this suggestion - this solution works for us and will facilitate the swift progression of the Tonga and PNG agreements.  \nHave a nice evening, Elizabeth \n\nOn Thu, Feb 6, 2025 at 5:14 PM Birgit Kuba <bkuba@worldbank.org> wrote:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nDear Elizabeth,\n \nThank you for getting back to us with these comments. We understand your concerns.\n\n \nIn the interest of moving the two Technical Assistance (TA) agreements in Tonga and PNG forward, we suggest reverting to the definitions of the “old” UNOPS TA template, and not modifying sections outside the\n E&S General Condition and E&S table in the Annex. The “old” UNOPS TA template includes a definition of “Contractor”, which includes “implementing partners” and “partner organizations”. If we go with this, the E&S General Condition and the E&S table in the\n Annex would then just refer to “Contractors”. Does this sound like a good way forward to you?\n\n \nWe will have further opportunity to discuss the definitions in more substance once the new TA SFA template will be ready to be shared.\n\n \nWe look forward to hearing from you.\nBest wishes,\nBirgit \n \n\n\nFrom: Elizabeth DUBY <elizabethdu@unops.org>\n\n\nSent: Thursday, February 6, 2025 3:26 PM\n\nTo: Birgit Kuba <bkuba@worldbank.org>\n\nCc: Million Fikre <mfikre@worldbank.org>; Christine BOWERS <christinebo@unops.org>; Alistair Peter SOMERVILLE <alistairs@unops.org>; Gamila Kassem <gkassem1@worldbank.org>; Andrew C. Kircher <akircher1@worldbank.org>; Xiaoping Li <xli2@worldbank.org>;\n Alison Micheli <amicheli@worldbank.org>\n\nSubject: Re: Retrofitted Technical Assistance SFA for Engagements at an Advanced Stage of Preparation\n\n\n \n[External]\n\n\n\nDear Birgit, \nThank you for your explanation of the changes and the rationale behind them. We’ve gone through the revisions and have a few adjustments we’d like to propose.\nFirst, with respect to the definition of Non-Staff Personnel, we would like to account for the fact that a large number of our personnel are International Contractor Agreement (ICA) - a type of contract modality used to engage individuals as\n consultants or contractors for specific projects or assignments. \nMoving down to Article 15 and 19 - this is the most significant issue for us as it presents a challenge under Articles 100 and 101 of the UN Charter. These articles establish member states’ commitment to respecting the exclusivity of the international\n character of the UN, including an obligation not to seek to influence the UN in the discharge of its responsibilities, as well as the duty to recruit personnel based on the highest standards. Sharing CVs deviates from this commitment, as it exposes UNOPS to\n scrutiny over its recruitment processes and the individuals selected. Instead, we propose sharing the TORs of the selected individuals. This would allow us to demonstrate that we have recruited personnel to perform the requested work, without inviting interference\n in our independent recruitment processes. Additionally, we have confidentiality obligations toward our personnel and should not be sharing their CVs without consent. \nFinally at Article 20, we would like to add a reference to UNOPS applying its established regulations, rules, policies, and procedures to ensure a common understanding of the standard being applied by UNOPS in making its determinations.  We also propose\n to align Article 20 with the revision to Article 15, meaning that instead of sharing CVs, we would share terms of reference.\nI have attached the draft with track changes and comments for easy reference.  Let me know if you'd like to discuss further. \nBest regards, \n\nElizabeth \n\n \n\n\nOn Mon, Feb 3, 2025 at 11:18 AM Birgit Kuba <bkuba@worldbank.org> wrote:\n\n\n\n\n\nDear Elizabeth, \n\n \nThank you for your message. I would like to jump in to provide a few clarifications on the technical/legal aspects – see below in\ngreen. \n \nPlease let us know as soon as you complete your review of the Tonga agreement.\n \nBest wishes,\nBirgit\n\n \n\n\nFrom: Elizabeth DUBY <elizabethdu@unops.org>\n\n\nSent: Friday, January 31, 2025 4:26 PM\n\nTo: Million Fikre <mfikre@worldbank.org>\n\nCc: Christine BOWERS <christinebo@unops.org>; Alistair Peter SOMERVILLE <alistairs@unops.org>; Birgit Kuba <bkuba@worldbank.org>;\n Gamila Kassem <gkassem1@worldbank.org>; Andrew C. Kircher <akircher1@worldbank.org>; Xiaoping Li <xli2@worldbank.org>;\n Alison Micheli <amicheli@worldbank.org>\n\nSubject: Re: Retrofitted Technical Assistance SFA for Engagements at an Advanced Stage of Preparation\n\n\n \n[External]\n\n\n\n\nDear Million,\nThank you very much for your email and for the information regarding the Tonga and Papua New Guinea projects.\nSince there were a few adjustments beyond Article 21 incorporated into this template, we are conducting a review and will get back to you very soon.\nIn addition to inserting the E&S Covenant (article 21, or in this case, article 18) in the General Conditions and the E&S section (section 3) in Annex 1, we updated the definitions (Contractor, Implementing Partner, Staff, Non-Staff\n Personnel, Personal Data), which had been updated to clarify the scope of E&S obligations. To ensure consistency throughout the agreement, we changed the references to these definitions throughout. This does not entail any substantive changes of requirements.\n   \nAs you indicated that the project for Papua New Guinea was approved under the Bank’s previous environmental and social safeguards, I would like to clarify whether you are referring to the safeguards that belong in the July template, now superseded by the\n November version. When referring to the Bank’s previous environmental and social safeguards, we are referring to the Bank’s E&S policies that were in force prior to the Bank’s Environmental and Social Framework (ESF) being adopted\n and becoming effective for new projects in late 2018. The PNG Project, under which the Government is engaging UNOPS, was prepared under these “old” safeguard policies, which continue to be the applicable policy framework for this project.\n\nWhat this means in practice for the SFA with UNOPS is that the E&S Covenant (article 21) will refer to the “Environmental and Social Safeguard Policies” instead of the ESSs, otherwise the E&S Covenant remains the same.\n“Environmental and Social Risk Management. (a) The UN Partner shall provide the Technical Assistance in accordance with the\nBank’s Environmental and Social Safeguard Policies, and as outlined in Annex I, Section III\n(the “E&S Obligations”).\n\n(b) To this end, when the UN Partner engages Contractors and/or Implementing Partners in the provision of Technical Assistance under this Agreement, the UN\n Partner shall identify all parts of the E&S Obligations relevant to the provision of Technical Assistance to be carried out by the Contractors and/or Implementing Partners, which may include, inter alia, requirements for codes of conduct, security risk assessment\n and management, reporting on E&S implementation, and incident and accident notification and reporting. The UN Partner shall, in relation to each Contractor and/or Implementing Partner:\n\na.     \nensure that the Contractor and/or Implementing Partner is contractually bound to comply with such E&S Obligations;\n\nb.    \nensure that the Contractor and/or Implementing Partner has the capacity to comply with such E&S Obligations;\n\n\nc.     \nmonitor the implementation of such E&S Obligations by the Contractor and/or Implementing Partner and require remedial action where necessary; and\n\nd.    \nat the Bank’s request, share with the Bank[1]\n the contracts entered into with the Contractors and/or Implementing Partners engaged in the provision of Technical Assistance, redacted to remove Personal Data and confidential business information, provided that such redactions do not impair the Bank's ability\n to review the E&S Obligations included in those contracts.”\nFor the E&S text and table in the Annex, the main point to note is that the old safeguard policies were narrower in their coverage of substantive topics as they did not include a policy on labor (so there will not be a reference\n to labor management procedures); they also did not require a Stakeholder Engagement Plan (so no reference to that either). Under the old safeguard policies there was also no Environmental and Social Commitment Plan (ESCP) so the chapeau to the table will only\n be referring to the Financing Agreement, and not to an ESCP. \nAdditionally, may I ask whether you anticipate any further changes to the TA template to be punished, beyond those already incorporated in the version you shared for Tonga?\nSince the new TA template has not come out yet, we are adopting the retrofitting approach to the Tonga and PNG agreements, meaning that the old agency-specific TA templates are being used, and are only being retrofitted with the\n new E&S sections (Article 21, E&S Annex, and definitions). We are making the changes to retrofit the PNG agreement now and plan to share it with you shortly.\n\nWe’d be happy to discuss this further at a convenient time.\n\n\nWith best regards, \n\n\nElizabeth\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nElizabeth Duby | Legal Advisor and Commercial Team Lead (Americas) | Legal\n Group | HQ | Washington DC | Mob: (202)391-2817  | IPAS\n Solution Portal  | www.unops.org |\n \n\nKeep up-to-date with UNOPS. Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube Subscribe\n to our external newsletter in English, French or Spanish\n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n\nOn Fri, 31 Jan 2025 at 14:56, Million Fikre <mfikre@worldbank.org> wrote:\n\n\n\n\nDear colleagues,\nFurther to my message, I would like to clarify that the proposed TA SFA would only be for Tonga; the PNG agreement would need to be treated somewhat differently given that the underlying\n project was approved under the Bank’s previous environmental and social safeguards. We can explain what this means in practice, but for now wanted to flag this so that there is not confusion later.\nAs it stands, we await your review of the Tonga SFA \nWith thanks,\nMillion\n \n\n\nFrom: Million Fikre\n\n\nSent: Wednesday, January 29, 2025 4:59 PM\n\nTo: Christine BOWERS <christinebo@unops.org>; Elizabeth DUBY <elizabethdu@unops.org>; Alistair Peter SOMERVILLE <alistairs@unops.org>\n\nCc: Birgit Kuba <bkuba@worldbank.org>; Gamila Kassem <gkassem1@worldbank.org>; Andrew C. Kircher <akircher1@worldbank.org>;\n Xiaoping Li <xli2@worldbank.org>; Alison Micheli <amicheli@worldbank.org>\n\nSubject: Retrofitted Technical Assistance SFA for Engagements at an Advanced Stage of Preparation\n\n\n \nDear colleagues,\n\n \nPlease find attached for you review a retrofitted Technical Assistance SFA for engagements at an advanced stage of preparation – PNG and Tonga immediately come to mind. In the document,\n you will find that E&S general condition is consistent with the Outputs SFA. Moreover, please note that changes to definitions are meant to ensure consistency throughout the document.\n\n \nWith your agreement, we would be able to advance the agreements for PNG and Tonga, with the understanding that our respective task teams will continue to finalize the exact details\n of the proposed engagements. In parallel, our combined team in OPCS and LEG will continue efforts to update and circulate the TA SFA template for new engagements.\n \nStanding by with thanks,\n\nMillion\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n[1]\n Information and documents, including contracts and any incident or accident reports, provided to the Bank will be: (a) marked as “Information Provided by Member Countries or Third Parties in Confidence” pursuant to the Bank’s Access to Information Policy,\n and (b) assigned the security classification of “confidential” pursuant to the Bank Directive on Information Classification and Control, i.e., the information will only be: (i) disseminated within the Bank on a “need to know” basis; and (ii) shared with third\n parties on agreement with the UN Partner. \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n-- \n\n\n\nElizabeth Duby | Legal Advisor | IPAS | HQ | Copenhagen | Mob:\n +45 30177661  | IPAS\n Solution Portal  | www.unops.org |\n\n \n\nKeep up-to-date with UNOPS. Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube Subscribe\n to our external newsletter in English, French or Spanish\n \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n-- \nElizabeth Duby | Legal Advisor | IPAS | HQ | Copenhagen | Mob:\n+45 30177661  | IPAS\nSolution Portal  | www.unops.org |\n\n \n\nKeep up-to-date with UNOPS. Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube Subscribe to our\nexternal newsletter in English, French or Spanish",
                        Location = null,
                        GmailThreadId = null,
                        GmailMessageId = "00TQx00000LJcpKMAT",
                        EmailAddresses = new List<string> { "mfikre@worldbank.org", "amicheli@worldbank.org", "gkassem1@worldbank.org", "elizabethdu@unops.org", "christinebo@unops.org", "xli2@worldbank.org", "akircher1@worldbank.org", "bkuba@worldbank.org", "alistairs@unops.org" },
