                        Date = DateTime.Parse("2025-08-07").ToUniversalTime(),
                        Subject = "Email: Informal conversation with Denmark on Executive Board and other matters",
                        Description = "To: kirstined@unops.org; sonjalk@unops.org; hilaryb@unops.org; rahulm@unops.org; freyavg@unops.org; orenng@unops.org; karls@unops.org; thomasl@unops.org; yngvilf@unops.org; svene@unops.org; markuso@unops.org; carolinapo@unops.org\nCC: eo.cph@unops.org; plg.lo@unops.org; arnauds@unops.org; djenebas@unops.org\nBCC: \nAttachment: --none--\n\nSubject: Informal conversation with Denmark on Executive Board and other matters\nBody:\nDear colleagues, \nEarlier today I met with the Deputy Director for the Multilateral Department, the UN team as well as the Deputy Director for the Financial Control Department at the Danish MFA to informally discuss the upcoming Executive Board and other topics. \nThe Danish MFA praised the collaboration with UNOPS - noting the recent successful CEB in Copenhagen, common efforts to increase awareness about the UN and UNOPS in Denmark, engagements in Ukraine, Afghanistan, Santiago Network, Somalia, etc. and UNOPS' unique ability to showcase tangible results of the UN work in difficult circumstances.  \nI conveyed our key messages on the 4 key topics ahead of the August EB, noting that the Danes had not yet studied the vast amount of documents provided by UNOPS and the other UN agencies in advance of the EB. The MFA pointed towards the information overload to be an increasing worry that they had hoped could be solved in relation to the JIU recommendations - although they did not have much confidence in the process. \nThe Danish MFA would no longer allocate as many internal resources to follow UNOPS EB matters, as they considered the issues following the S3i revelations to be closed and as they did not allocate core contributions to UNOPS. Formally the matter would be closed by a report from the MFA to the Danish Court of Auditors in the coming months. \nThe Danish focus would henceforth be on the specific projects/engagements (which they expected to increase) and on a global perspective on UNOPS risk management and the KPMG follow-up recommendations in relation to EB matters. The MFA Financial Control Division would therefore be at leading the Danish engagements in EB matters. \nIt was noted that the government continued to receive a number of parliamentary questions relating to UNOPS (UNBOA recommendations on the inappropriate use of financial hedging instruments, DUNDEX, status on recovering missing S3i funds, etc.).   \nDenmark would continue to attend EB sessions due to UNOPS being headquartered in Copenhagen and would seek to act as a goodwill ambassador for UNOPS - although not being at the forefront of the WEOG group. \nIn general Denmark was supportive of the UNOPS Strategic Plan - praising the efforts to ensure that UNOPS would stick to the core mandate and work towards being better at measuring outcome better rather than only output. However, Denmark noted that UNOPS was a very output based organisation producing very tangible results, which UNOPS should not shy away from as it remained one of the strongest competencies / selling points of UNOPS.  \nDenmark was aware of the critical voices within the WEOG group of UNOPS Mission Hubs - but found that EB Members had understood that this was merely UNOPS internal knowledge sharing hubs. Denmark - and other WEOG members - did however continue to find the term \"mission\" unfortunate as it had an unfortunate link to \"normative\". \nDenmark found it unlikely that the EB would approve UNOPS' full request to allocate additional 60M USD from the reserves to the PID as this was a very large amount of funds over a long period of time to a very large IT project. The Danes considered it more likely with partial allocations of funds during the time period - with regular reporting to the EB. UNOPS was encouraged to develop a plan B - containing solid arguments for how much funding allocation was strictly needed for now to ensure predictability in planning - while additional funding could be allocated at a later period.\nFinally it was agreed to initiate drafting of a standard template for engagements between Denmark and UNOPS to ease the workload in the negotiation process for colleagues and as there was an increasing amount of engagements between Denmark and UNOPS. \nBest,Asbjørn \n--\nAsbjørn Brink| Head of Northern Europe Liaison Office | Partnerships and Liaison Group | HQ | Copenhagen, Denmark | Tel: +45 40 80 36 54 | Follow us on social media and subscribe to our newsletter. | www.unops.org",
